PROGRESSIVE_THEORY_DICT = {('EM', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            1.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Propositions & Basic Logic Connectives\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>What is a Proposition?</b> A declarative sentence that is either strictly True (T) or strictly False (F), but never both.<br/>• <b>The 5 Fundamental Connectives:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>NOT (~P):</b> Reverses the truth value. If P is True, ~P is False.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>AND (P ∧ Q):</b> True ONLY when BOTH P and Q are True (conjunction).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>OR (P ∨ Q):</b> True if AT LEAST ONE of P or Q is True. False only when both are False (disjunction).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Conditional / Implication (P → Q):</b> Reads \'If P, then Q\'. False ONLY when P is True and Q is False.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Biconditional (P ↔ Q):</b> True when P and Q have identical truth values (both True or both False).<br/>• <b>Equivalence Identity:</b> (P → Q) is logically equivalent to (~P ∨ Q).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: P → Q is False ONLY when P = True and Q = False. In all other three cases (T→T, F→T, F→F), the implication is ALWAYS TRUE!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            1.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Contrapositive, Converse, and Inverse\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • For any conditional statement <b>P → Q</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Contrapositive (~Q → ~P):</b> \'If NOT Q, then NOT P\'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Equivalence:</b> (P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P). Always holds true in every possible case!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Converse (Q → P):</b> \'If Q, then P\'. NOT equivalent to the original statement.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Inverse (~P → ~Q):</b> \'If NOT P, then NOT Q\'. Equivalent to the Converse, NOT the original.<br/>• <b>GATE Shortcut:</b> When proving an implication theorem in GATE, proving its contrapositive is often 10x easier!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: A statement and its Contrapositive always share the exact same truth table: (P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P). Converse and Inverse are equal to each other, but NOT to the original statement.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            1.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Quantifiers: For All (∀) vs There Exists (∃)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Universal Quantifier (∀x):</b> \'For all x\'. True only if the statement holds for every single element in the universe.<br/>• <b>Existential Quantifier (∃x):</b> \'There exists x\'. True if at least one matching element exists in the universe.<br/>• <b>Negation Rules (De Morgan for Quantifiers):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- ~[ ∀x P(x) ] ≡ ∃x [ ~P(x) ] (Negating \'All birds fly\' gives \'There exists at least one bird that does NOT fly\').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- ~[ ∃x P(x) ] ≡ ∀x [ ~P(x) ] (Negating \'Someone got 100\' gives \'Everyone scored not 100\').<br/>• <b>Order of Quantifiers Matters:</b> ∀x ∃y P(x, y) is NOT the same as ∃y ∀x P(x, y)!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Negating \'All X are Y\' is \'There is at least one X that is NOT Y\'. It is NEVER \'No X are Y\'!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            1.4\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Counting Relations on a Set of Size n\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Let set A contain <b>n</b> elements. The Cartesian product A × A contains <b>n²</b> ordered pairs.<br/>• <b>Formulas to memorize for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Total possible relations = <b>2^(n²)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Total Reflexive relations = <b>2^(n² - n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Total Symmetric relations = <b>2^(n(n+1)/2)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Total Reflexive and Symmetric relations = <b>2^(n(n-1)/2)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. Total Equivalence relations = Counted by <b>Bell Numbers</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;B(1) = 1, B(2) = 2, B(3) = 5, B(4) = 15, B(5) = 52.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For n = 4 elements, total equivalence relations = Bell Number B(4) = 15. Memorize: B(1)=1, B(2)=2, B(3)=5, B(4)=15, B(5)=52.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Counting Symmetric Relations for n = 4\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given set S = {a, b, c, d}. How many symmetric relations can be defined on set S?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Identify number of elements n = 4.<br/>• Step 2: Formula for total symmetric relations is 2^(n(n+1)/2).<br/>• Step 3: Exponent = (4 × (4 + 1)) / 2 = (4 × 5) / 2 = 20 / 2 = 10.<br/>• Step 4: Compute 2^10 = 1024.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total Symmetric Relations = 1024.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          A relation can be BOTH symmetric and anti-symmetric at the same time (e.g. Identity relation on any set)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Empty relation on an empty set is reflexive, but empty relation on a non-empty set is NOT reflexive.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Vacuous truth trap: The empty relation is vacuously symmetric and transitive!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Wi-Fi Login & Father\'s Promise\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Proposition: Like entering a Wi-Fi password—it is strictly Correct (True) or Incorrect (False). Questions or commands are not propositions.\n• Implication (P -> Q): Dad promises \'If you get AIR 1 (P), I will buy you a bike (Q)\'. He only breaks his word if you get AIR 1 and he refuses to buy the bike (T -> F = False). If you don\'t get AIR 1, whatever he does never breaks the promise (Vacuous Truth).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('EM', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            2.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Pigeonhole Principle (PHP) & Permutations\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Basic PHP:</b> If <b>k + 1</b> or more objects are placed into <b>k</b> boxes, then at least one box must contain 2 or more objects.<br/>• <b>Generalized PHP:</b> If <b>N</b> objects are placed into <b>k</b> boxes, then at least one box contains at least <b>⌈N / k⌉</b> objects.<br/>• <b>Permutations vs Combinations:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Permutation (Order matters): P(n, r) = n! / (n - r)!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Combination (Order does NOT matter): C(n, r) = n! / [ r! (n - r)! ]<br/>• <b>Circular Permutations:</b> Arranging n distinct objects around a circular table = <b>(n - 1)!</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To guarantee at least m items in the same box out of k boxes, you need at least k(m - 1) + 1 items in total!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            2.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Solving Linear Recurrence Relations\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Homogeneous Recurrence:</b> a_n = c1·a_{n-1} + c2·a_{n-2}.<br/>• <b>Step-by-step Solution:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Form characteristic equation: r² - c1·r - c2 = 0.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Case 1 (Distinct Real Roots r1 ≠ r2):</b> General solution is a_n = A·(r1)^n + B·(r2)^n.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Case 2 (Repeated Roots r1 = r2 = r):</b> General solution is a_n = (A + B·n)·r^n.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Substitute base cases (a0, a1) to solve for constants A and B.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If characteristic root r has multiplicity m, the solution term is (A0 + A1·n + ... + A_{m-1}·n^{m-1})·r^n.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            2.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Graph Theory Fundamentals & Handshaking Theorem\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Handshaking Lemma:</b> In any undirected graph: <b>∑ deg(v) = 2|E|</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum of all vertex degrees is always EVEN.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Corollary:</b> In every graph, the number of vertices with ODD degree is ALWAYS EVEN!<br/>• <b>Complete Graph (K_n):</b> Every pair of vertices has an edge. Total edges = <b>n(n - 1) / 2</b>.<br/>• <b>Bipartite Graph (K_{m, n}):</b> Vertices split into two independent sets. Max edges = <b>m × n</b>.<br/>• <b>Cycle Graph (C_n):</b> Regular graph of degree 2 with n vertices and n edges.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: A graph is bipartite if and only if it contains NO odd-length cycles! If a graph has a triangle (C_3) or pentagon (C_5), it CANNOT be bipartite.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            2.4\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Planar Graphs & Spanning Trees\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Euler\'s Planar Formula:</b> For any connected planar graph with V vertices, E edges, and R regions:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>V - E + R = 2</b>.<br/>• <b>Planarity Edge Bounds:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- For any connected planar graph with V ≥ 3: <b>E ≤ 3V - 6</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If triangle-free (no cycle of length 3): <b>E ≤ 2V - 4</b>.<br/>• <b>Kuratowski\'s Theorem:</b> A graph is planar if and only if it does not contain a subgraph homeomorphic to K_5 or K_{3,3}.<br/>• <b>Trees:</b> A connected acyclic graph on n vertices has exactly <b>n - 1 edges</b>. Cayley\'s formula: Total labeled trees on n vertices = <b>n^(n - 2)</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: K_5 (complete graph on 5 vertices) and K_{3,3} (complete bipartite utility graph) are the two minimal non-planar graphs! E ≤ 3V - 6 is a necessary condition for planarity, not sufficient.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Planar Graph Regions Calculation\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A connected planar graph has 10 vertices and each vertex has degree 3. How many faces (regions) does this graph divide the plane into?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Use Handshaking Theorem to find E: ∑ deg(v) = 2E.<br/>• Step 2: Here 10 vertices each have degree 3. Total degree sum = 10 × 3 = 30.<br/>• Step 3: 2E = 30 ⇒ E = 15 edges.<br/>• Step 4: Apply Euler\'s Planar Formula: V - E + R = 2.<br/>• Step 5: Substitute V = 10, E = 15: 10 - 15 + R = 2 ⇒ -5 + R = 2 ⇒ R = 7.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total Regions (Faces) R = 7.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Euler\'s formula V - E + R = 2 requires the graph to be CONNECTED. If disconnected with k components: V - E + R = 1 + k.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Bounded regions vs unbounded regions: R includes the 1 outer infinite region! Bounded faces = R - 1.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Socks in the Dark & Network Handshakes\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Pigeonhole Principle: If a drawer has black and blue socks, pulling out 3 socks guarantees at least 1 matching pair because 3 items go into 2 categories (color buckets).\n• Handshaking Theorem: At any party, when two people shake hands, 1 handshake adds 2 to the sum of handshakes. That is why the sum of degrees in any graph is always an even number!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('EM', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            3.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Matrix Determinants, Rank & Inverses\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Rank of a Matrix ρ(A):</b> Maximum number of linearly independent rows (or columns).<br/>• <b>Key Rank Properties:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- For an m × n matrix: <b>ρ(A) ≤ min(m, n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- ρ(A × B) ≤ min(ρ(A), ρ(B)).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- ρ(A + B) ≤ ρ(A) + ρ(B).<br/>• <b>Inverse Condition:</b> A square matrix A is invertible (non-singular) if and only if <b>det(A) ≠ 0</b> (full rank).<br/>• <b>Determinant Properties:</b> det(A × B) = det(A) · det(B); det(k · A_{n×n}) = k^n · det(A); det(A^T) = det(A).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For an n × n matrix, scaling all elements by constant k multiplies determinant by k^n: det(k·A) = k^n · det(A)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            3.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            System of Linear Equations (AX = B) Consistency\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Consider system <b>AX = B</b> with n variables. Let [A | B] be the augmented matrix.<br/>• <b>Step-by-Step Consistency Test:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Inconsistent (No Solution):</b> If <b>ρ(A) ≠ ρ(A | B)</b> (i.e. ρ(A) < ρ(A | B)).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Consistent (Unique Solution):</b> If <b>ρ(A) = ρ(A | B) = n</b> (full rank equal to number of unknowns).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Consistent (Infinitely Many Solutions):</b> If <b>ρ(A) = ρ(A | B) = r < n</b>. Number of free variables = <b>n - r</b>.<br/>• <b>Homogeneous System (AX = 0):</b> Always consistent (trivial solution X = 0). Has non-trivial solutions if and only if <b>det(A) = 0</b> (ρ(A) < n).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Homogeneous system AX = 0 has non-zero (non-trivial) solutions IF AND ONLY IF det(A) = 0 (rows are linearly dependent).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            3.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Eigenvalues & Eigenvectors (The 5 Golden Properties)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Characteristic equation: <b>det(A - λI) = 0</b>.<br/>• <b>The 5 Golden Exam Properties:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Sum of Eigenvalues:</b> Equal to the <b>Trace of A</b> (sum of main diagonal elements): ∑ λ_i = Tr(A).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Product of Eigenvalues:</b> Equal to the <b>Determinant of A</b>: ∏ λ_i = det(A).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Triangular Matrices:</b> For upper triangular, lower triangular, or diagonal matrices, eigenvalues are SIMPLY the main diagonal elements!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. If λ is an eigenvalue of A, then:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- A^k has eigenvalue <b>λ^k</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- A^(-1) has eigenvalue <b>1 / λ</b> (if λ ≠ 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- (A + cI) has eigenvalue <b>λ + c</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Symmetric Matrices:</b> Real symmetric matrices always have strictly REAL eigenvalues.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 90% of GATE eigenvalue questions, you DO NOT need to solve the cubic equation! Just check Trace = Sum(λ) and Det = Product(λ) against the 4 options!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            3.4\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Cayley-Hamilton Theorem & Matrix Powers\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Theorem Statement:</b> Every square matrix satisfies its own characteristic equation.<br/>• If characteristic equation is <b>λ² - 5λ + 6 = 0</b>, then matrix satisfies: <b>A² - 5A + 6I = 0</b>.<br/>• <b>Applications in GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Finding A^(-1):</b> Multiply equation by A^(-1): A - 5I + 6A^(-1) = 0 ⇒ <b>A^(-1) = (5I - A) / 6</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Computing High Powers (A^8, A^100):</b> Reduce high polynomial powers modulo the characteristic polynomial!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Multiply the characteristic equation by A^(-1) to obtain the inverse without ever computing cofactors or adjoints!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Finding Eigenvalues using Trace and Determinant\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Find the eigenvalues of matrix A = [[2, 1], [1, 2]].\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Calculate Trace(A) = sum of main diagonal = 2 + 2 = 4.<br/>• Step 2: Calculate Det(A) = (2 × 2) - (1 × 1) = 4 - 1 = 3.<br/>• Step 3: Two eigenvalues λ1, λ2 satisfy: λ1 + λ2 = 4 and λ1 · λ2 = 3.<br/>• Step 4: Two numbers that add to 4 and multiply to 3 are 1 and 3.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Eigenvalues are λ1 = 3, λ2 = 1.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          det(A + B) is NOT equal to det(A) + det(B)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent, but eigenvectors for repeated eigenvalues may or may not be independent.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: 3D Graphics Rotation & Bridge Resonance\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Matrices: In video games, moving and rotating a 3D camera is simply multiplying 3D coordinate vectors by 4x4 transformation matrices.\n• Eigenvalues: The natural vibration frequencies of a bridge or building. When earthquake vibrations match an eigenvalue, catastrophic resonance occurs!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('EM', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            4.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Limits & L\'Hôpital\'s Rule\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>L\'Hôpital\'s Rule:</b> Applicable ONLY for indeterminate forms <b>0 / 0</b> or <b>±∞ / ±∞</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;lim_{x→a} [ f(x) / g(x) ] = lim_{x→a} [ f\'(x) / g\'(x) ].<br/>• <b>Key Standard Limits for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- lim_{x→0} [ sin(x) / x ] = 1.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- lim_{x→0} [ (e^x - 1) / x ] = 1.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- lim_{x→0} [ ln(1 + x) / x ] = 1.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- lim_{n→∞} [ 1 + (a / n) ]^n = <b>e^a</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Always verify that the expression actually evaluates to 0/0 or ∞/∞ before taking derivatives! Applying L\'Hôpital to a determinate form (like 0/1) gives completely wrong answers.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            4.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Maxima, Minima & Mean Value Theorems\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Finding Critical Points:</b> Set first derivative f\'(x) = 0.<br/>• <b>Second Derivative Test:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If f\'\'(x) < 0: Local <b>Maximum</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If f\'\'(x) > 0: Local <b>Minimum</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If f\'\'(x) = 0: Test inconclusive (inflection point candidate).<br/>• <b>Rolle\'s Theorem:</b> If f(x) is continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b), with f(a) = f(b), then there exists at least one c ∈ (a, b) where <b>f\'(c) = 0</b>.<br/>• <b>Lagrange Mean Value Theorem (LMVT):</b> f\'(c) = [ f(b) - f(a) ] / (b - a).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On a closed interval [a, b], global absolute extrema can occur at critical points inside OR at the interval endpoints x = a and x = b. Always check endpoints!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            4.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Conditional Probability & Bayes\' Theorem\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Conditional Probability:</b> P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B).<br/>• <b>Independent Events:</b> Events A and B are independent if and only if <b>P(A ∩ B) = P(A) · P(B)</b>.<br/>• <b>Bayes\' Rule:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>P(A_i | B) = [ P(B | A_i) · P(A_i) ] / ∑ [ P(B | A_j) · P(A_j) ]</b>.<br/>• <b>Mutually Exclusive vs Independent:</b> If two non-zero events are mutually exclusive (P(A ∩ B) = 0), they CANNOT be independent!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Mutually exclusive means they cannot happen together (disjoint). Independent means occurrence of one does not affect the probability of the other. They are completely different concepts!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            4.4\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Probability Distributions (Binomial, Poisson, Normal)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Binomial Distribution:</b> P(X = k) = C(n, k) · p^k · (1 - p)^{n - k}.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Mean = <b>n · p</b>; Variance = <b>n · p · (1 - p)</b>.<br/>• <b>Poisson Distribution:</b> Rare events with rate λ: P(X = k) = (e^(-λ) · λ^k) / k!.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Unique Property:</b> Mean = <b>λ</b> and Variance = <b>λ</b> (Mean equals Variance!).<br/>• <b>Exponential Distribution:</b> Continuous waiting time: f(x) = λ·e^(-λx).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Memoryless property: P(X > s + t | X > s) = P(X > t).<br/>• <b>Uniform Distribution on [a, b]:</b> Mean = (a + b) / 2; Variance = <b>(b - a)² / 12</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Poisson distribution, Mean = Variance = λ. If an exam question says \'the variance of a Poisson variable is 4\', the mean is immediately 4 and λ = 4!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Bayes\' Theorem in Defective Item Screening\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Machine A produces 60% of items with 2% defect rate. Machine B produces 40% of items with 4% defect rate. An item is selected at random and found defective. What is the probability it was produced by Machine B?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: P(A) = 0.60, P(B) = 0.40.<br/>• Step 2: P(D | A) = 0.02, P(D | B) = 0.04.<br/>• Step 3: Total Probability of defective item P(D) = P(A)·P(D|A) + P(B)·P(D|B) = (0.60 × 0.02) + (0.40 × 0.04) = 0.012 + 0.016 = 0.028.<br/>• Step 4: By Bayes\' Theorem: P(B | D) = [ P(B) · P(D | B) ] / P(D) = 0.016 / 0.028 = 16 / 28 = 4 / 7 ≈ 0.5714.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ P(Machine B | Defective) = 4/7 (approx 57.14%).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Variance can NEVER be negative! Var(X) = E[X²] - (E[X])² ≥ 0.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Scaling property: Var(aX + b) = a² · Var(X). The constant b adds zero to variance!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Medical Test Accuracy & Coin Flips\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Bayes\' Theorem: A medical test is 99% accurate, but a disease is rare (1 in 10,000). If you test positive, the chance you actually have the disease is only ~1%! Because false positives from the healthy 9,999 vastly outnumber true positives.\n• Poisson Distribution: Modeling rare events over time, such as server web requests per minute or customer arrivals at an ATM.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            5.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Signed Number Ranges for n bits\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • For an <b>n-bit</b> signed integer representation:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Unsigned:</b> Range is <b>0 to 2^n - 1</b> (all bits used for magnitude).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Sign-Magnitude:</b> Range is <b>-(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1)</b>. (Has two representations of zero: +0 and -0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>1\'s Complement:</b> Range is <b>-(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1)</b>. (Also has two zeros: +0 and -0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>2\'s Complement:</b> Range is <b>-2^(n-1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1)</b>. (Has only ONE unique zero!).<br/>• <b>Asymmetry in 2\'s Complement:</b> For n = 8 bits, range is <b>-128 to +127</b>. Notice -128 has no positive counterpart in 8 bits!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 2\'s complement with n bits, the minimum negative number is -2^(n-1). It has NO positive counterpart (+2^(n-1) causes overflow). To find 2\'s complement fast: invert all bits and add 1, OR copy bits from right to left up to the first \'1\', then flip all remaining bits!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            5.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            2\'s Complement Overflow Detection\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>When does Overflow occur?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Adding two POSITIVE numbers gives a NEGATIVE result.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Adding two NEGATIVE numbers gives a POSITIVE result.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Adding a positive and a negative number NEVER causes overflow!<br/>• <b>Hardware Detection Rule:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Overflow = C_in ⊕ C_out</b> (XOR of carry-in and carry-out of the most significant bit MSB).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If C_in ≠ C_out, overflow has definitely occurred!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In signed arithmetic, carry-out from MSB does NOT mean overflow! Overflow occurs if and only if C_{in_MSB} ⊕ C_{out_MSB} = 1.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            5.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            IEEE-754 Single Precision (32-bit) Floating Point\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • The 32 bits are divided into 3 distinct fields:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Sign bit (S):</b> 1 bit (0 for positive, 1 for negative).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Exponent (E):</b> 8 bits with <b>Bias = 127</b>. Stored E = actual exponent e + 127.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Mantissa / Fraction (M):</b> 23 bits representing fractional part with implicit leading 1: <b>1.M</b>.<br/>• <b>Value Formula:</b> (-1)^S × 1.M × 2^(E - 127).<br/>• <b>Special Bit Patterns:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If E = 255 and M = 0: Represents <b>±Infinity</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If E = 255 and M ≠ 0: Represents <b>NaN (Not a Number)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If E = 0 and M = 0: Represents <b>±0</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If E = 0 and M ≠ 0: <b>Denormalized number</b> with value (-1)^S × 0.M × 2^(-126).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For normalized IEEE-754 numbers, the leading \'1\' before the decimal point is implicit (not stored in memory). The smallest normalized positive number is 1.0 × 2^(-126) (E = 1).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: IEEE-754 Representation of -13.625\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Convert the real decimal number -13.625 into IEEE-754 single precision 32-bit binary representation.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Sign is negative ⇒ S = 1.<br/>• Step 2: Convert integer part: 13 = 1101 in binary.<br/>• Step 3: Convert fractional part: 0.625 = 0.5 + 0.125 = 0.101 in binary.<br/>• Step 4: Combine: 13.625 = 1101.101 in binary.<br/>• Step 5: Normalize to 1.M form: 1101.101 = 1.101101 × 2^3.<br/>• Step 6: Actual exponent e = 3. Stored exponent E = 3 + 127 = 130 = 10000010 in binary.<br/>• Step 7: Mantissa M = 101101 padded with zeros to 23 bits.<br/>• Step 8: Combine (S | E | M): 1 | 10000010 | 10110100000000000000000. In Hex: C15A0000.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Hex representation: 0xC15A0000.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Forgetting to add bias 127 to the exponent!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Double precision IEEE-754 uses 64 bits: 1 sign bit, 11 exponent bits (bias = 1023), and 52 mantissa bits.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Odometer Wrap-Around & Game Integer Overflow\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • 2\'s Complement: Like a car odometer running backwards from 00000 to 99999. In an 8-bit computer, counting backwards from 00000000 gives 11111111, which represents -1.\n• Overflow: In early arcade games, when a score reached 255 (max for 8 bits) and added 1, it rolled over to 0! That is integer overflow.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            6.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Boolean Algebra Laws & Consensus Theorem\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>De Morgan\'s Laws:</b> (A + B)\' = A\' · B\' &nbsp;and&nbsp; (A · B)\' = A\' + B\'.<br/>• <b>Distributive Law:</b> A + BC = (A + B)(A + C). (Crucial for factoring expressions!).<br/>• <b>Consensus Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AB + A\'C + BC = AB + A\'C</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Variable A appears in true form and complemented form (A, A\').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The term <b>BC</b> is redundant (consensus of AB and A\'C) and can be completely eliminated!<br/>• <b>Dual Consensus Form:</b> (A + B)(A\' + C)(B + C) = (A + B)(A\' + C).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In consensus theorem AB + A\'C + BC = AB + A\'C, look for the variable that appears complemented (A and A\'). The term formed by the remaining two variables (BC) is ALWAYS REDUNDANT!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            6.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Universal Gates: Minimum NAND/NOR Implementation Counts\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • NAND and NOR are universal gates because any boolean function can be realized using only NAND or only NOR.<br/>• <b>Minimum Gate Counts to Memorize for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>NOT:</b> 1 NAND | 1 NOR<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>AND:</b> 2 NAND | 3 NOR<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>OR:</b> 3 NAND | 2 NOR<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>XOR:</b> 4 NAND | 5 NOR<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>XNOR:</b> 5 NAND | 4 NOR\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: XOR requires 4 NAND gates (or 5 NOR gates). XNOR requires 5 NAND gates (or 4 NOR gates). Notice the perfect symmetry: 4 vs 5!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            6.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            K-Map Minimization, Prime & Essential Prime Implicants\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Gray Code Ordering:</b> Cells in K-map are ordered 00, 01, 11, 10 so adjacent cells differ by exactly 1 bit.<br/>• <b>Implicant:</b> Any product term that covers one or more 1s of the function.<br/>• <b>Prime Implicant (PI):</b> A rectangle of size 2^k (1, 2, 4, 8, 16) that CANNOT be merged into a larger group.<br/>• <b>Essential Prime Implicant (EPI):</b> A Prime Implicant that covers at least one \'1\' that is NOT covered by any other prime implicant.<br/>• <b>Minimal SOP expression MUST contain ALL Essential Prime Implicants!</b>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every EPI is a PI, but not every PI is an EPI. To find EPIs fast: find a \'1\' that belongs to only ONE valid largest rectangle. That rectangle is an Essential Prime Implicant!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Boolean Function Minimization using Consensus\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Simplify the Boolean function F = X\'Y + XZ + YZ.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Check the structure of terms: X\'Y, XZ, YZ.<br/>• Step 2: Variable X appears complemented as X and X\'.<br/>• Step 3: The other two variables are Y and Z.<br/>• Step 4: The third term YZ is the consensus of X\'Y and XZ.<br/>• Step 5: By Consensus Theorem, YZ is redundant and eliminated: F = X\'Y + XZ.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Simplified function: F = X\'Y + XZ.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In K-maps, grouping 0s gives the minimized POS expression (F\'), not SOP!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Don\'t-care conditions (X) can be included in groups to make groups larger, but you NEVER need to cover all don\'t-cares.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Universal Factory Tool & Logic Simplification\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Universal Gates: Just as LEGO bricks can build any toy castle or spaceship, NAND and NOR gates alone can build any computer chip in existence!\n• K-Map: A visual puzzle where adjacent boxes differ by only 1 bit. Grouping neighboring 1s cancels out changing variables, shrinking circuit size.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            7.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Multiplexer (MUX) as a Universal Circuit\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • A <b>2^n : 1 MUX</b> has 2^n data inputs, n select lines, and 1 output.<br/>• <b>Implementing any n-variable function:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Using a <b>2^n : 1 MUX</b>: Connect all n variables directly to select lines. Connect truth table outputs (0 or 1) to data inputs. No extra gates needed!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Using a <b>2^(n-1) : 1 MUX</b>: Connect (n - 1) variables to select lines. The last variable X can provide data inputs as <b>0, 1, X, or X\'</b>. May need an inverter for X\'.<br/>• <b>Shannon\'s Expansion Theorem:</b> F(A, B, C) = A\' · F(0, B, C) + A · F(1, B, C).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Any Boolean function of n variables can be implemented using a single 2^(n-1) : 1 MUX with at most one external inverter!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            7.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Half Adder vs Full Adder Architecture\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Half Adder (HA):</b> Adds 2 single-bit numbers A and B.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = <b>A ⊕ B</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Carry = <b>A · B</b>.<br/>• <b>Full Adder (FA):</b> Adds 3 bits: A, B, and Carry-in C_in.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = <b>A ⊕ B ⊕ C_in</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Carry-out = <b>AB + BC_in + AC_in = AB + C_in(A ⊕ B)</b>.<br/>• <b>Construction:</b> 1 Full Adder can be constructed using exactly <b>2 Half Adders and 1 OR gate</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To build an n-bit Ripple Carry Adder, you need 1 Half Adder and (n - 1) Full Adders (or n Full Adders if initial carry is needed). Total delay = n × (carry propagation delay).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            7.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Decoders & Priority Encoders\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>n-to-2^n Decoder:</b> Converts n binary inputs into 2^n active minterm outputs.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any Boolean function can be implemented using a Decoder and an external OR gate (sum of minterms).<br/>• <b>Priority Encoder:</b> If multiple inputs are active simultaneously, the output represents the highest priority active input.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Includes a <b>Valid bit (V)</b>: V = 1 if at least one input is active; V = 0 if all inputs are inactive.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To construct an m-to-2^m decoder using smaller k-to-2^k decoders: Number of smaller decoders required = ⌈2^m / 2^k⌉ + tree levels.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Implementing Logic with 4:1 MUX\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Implement F(A, B, C) = ∑m(1, 2, 4, 7) using a 4:1 MUX where select lines S1 = A and S0 = B.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Write truth table for (A, B, C) and output F.<br/>• Step 2: Group by (A, B) pairs: (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1).<br/>• Step 3: For AB = 00: m0(C=0)->F=0, m1(C=1)->F=1 ⇒ I0 = C.<br/>• Step 4: For AB = 01: m2(C=0)->F=1, m3(C=1)->F=0 ⇒ I1 = C\'.<br/>• Step 5: For AB = 10: m4(C=0)->F=1, m5(C=1)->F=0 ⇒ I2 = C\'.<br/>• Step 6: For AB = 11: m6(C=0)->F=0, m7(C=1)->F=1 ⇒ I3 = C.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Inputs to 4:1 MUX are I0 = C, I1 = C\', I2 = C\', I3 = C.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Pay strict attention to which variable is MSB and LSB on the select lines (S1 = A, S0 = B vs S1 = B, S0 = A)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Active-low enables in decoders require invert logic before cascading.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Train Track Switch & Address Decoder\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Multiplexer (MUX): Like a railway switch operator who chooses which incoming track (data line) connects to the single outgoing main line, controlled by switch levers (select lines).\n• Decoder: Like a postal delivery worker who reads a numeric zip code and delivers the package to exactly one specific house door.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DL', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            8.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Flip-Flop Characteristic Equations & Truth Tables\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>SR Flip-Flop:</b> Q_{next} = S + R\'Q (Invalid state when S = 1, R = 1).<br/>• <b>JK Flip-Flop:</b> Q_{next} = J·Q\' + K\'·Q.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=0, K=0: Hold (Q).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=0, K=1: Reset (0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=1, K=0: Set (1).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=1, K=1: Toggle (Q\') - solves SR race condition!<br/>• <b>D Flip-Flop (Delay):</b> Q_{next} = <b>D</b> (data storage).<br/>• <b>T Flip-Flop (Toggle):</b> Q_{next} = <b>T ⊕ Q</b> (T=0: Hold, T=1: Toggle).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The characteristic equations must be on your fingertips: JK: Q_{next} = J·Q\' + K\'·Q | D: Q_{next} = D | T: Q_{next} = T ⊕ Q.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            8.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Timing Constraints: Setup Time, Hold Time & Max Clock Frequency\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Setup Time (t_setup):</b> Minimum time data input must remain stable BEFORE the active clock edge.<br/>• <b>Hold Time (t_hold):</b> Minimum time data input must remain stable AFTER the active clock edge.<br/>• <b>Minimum Clock Period (T_clk):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>T_clk ≥ t_{cq} + t_{comb} + t_{setup}</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- t_{cq} = Clock-to-Q delay of the launching flip-flop.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- t_{comb} = Maximum propagation delay of combinational logic between flip-flops.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- t_{setup} = Setup time of the receiving flip-flop.<br/>• <b>Maximum Clock Frequency:</b> f_{max} = 1 / T_{clk_min}.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To avoid Hold time violations: t_{cq_min} + t_{comb_min} ≥ t_{hold}. Notice that hold time is independent of clock frequency!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            8.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Counters: Asynchronous vs Synchronous, Ring & Johnson\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Asynchronous (Ripple) Counter:</b> Output of one flip-flop acts as clock for the next.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Total propagation delay = <b>n × t_{pd}</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Max clock frequency decreases rapidly as bit-width n increases.<br/>• <b>Synchronous Counter:</b> All flip-flops share the same master clock. Much faster! Delay = <b>t_{pd} + t_{gate}</b>.<br/>• <b>Shift Register Counters with n Flip-Flops:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Standard Binary Counter:</b> Has <b>2^n</b> unique states.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Ring Counter:</b> Output of last FF fed back to first. Has <b>n</b> unique states.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Johnson (Twisted Ring) Counter:</b> Inverted output of last FF fed back to first. Has <b>2n</b> unique states!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An n-flip-flop Johnson counter has 2n states, while an n-flip-flop Ring counter has only n states! Unused states: Ring has (2^n - n) unused states; Johnson has (2^n - 2n) unused states.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Calculating Maximum Clock Frequency\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A sequential circuit has flip-flop t_cq = 2 ns, t_setup = 1.5 ns, and combinational logic delay = 4.5 ns. What is the maximum clock frequency f_max?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Formula for minimum clock period: T_clk ≥ t_cq + t_comb + t_setup.<br/>• Step 2: Substitute values: T_clk_min = 2 ns + 4.5 ns + 1.5 ns = 8 ns.<br/>• Step 3: Maximum frequency f_max = 1 / T_clk_min = 1 / (8 × 10^(-9) s) = 125 MHz.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Maximum clock frequency = 125 MHz.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In negative edge-triggered ripple counter: connecting Q to clock gives an UP counter; connecting Q\' gives a DOWN counter!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Race-around condition in JK flip-flop occurs when J = 1, K = 1 and clock pulse width t_p > propagation delay t_pd in level-triggered latches. Solved by edge-triggering or master-slave configuration.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Stop-Motion Camera & Turnstile Counter\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Flip-Flop: Like a high-speed camera taking a photo strictly on the rising edge of the photographer\'s click. Inputs must stay stable before (setup time) and after (hold time) the snapshot.\n• Counter: Like an amusement park turnstile clicker that increments each time a visitor passes through, cycling back to zero when max capacity is reached.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            9.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Effective Address (EA) in Common Addressing Modes\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>What is Effective Address (EA)?</b> The actual physical address in main memory where the operand resides.<br/>• <b>Standard Addressing Modes Summary:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Immediate Mode:</b> Operand is part of the instruction itself. (Zero memory references!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Direct (Absolute) Mode:</b> Address field contains EA: <b>EA = Address</b>. (1 memory reference).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Indirect Mode:</b> Address field contains pointer to EA: <b>EA = M[Address]</b>. (2 memory references).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Register Mode:</b> Operand resides in a CPU register R: <b>EA = R</b>. (Zero memory references, very fast).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Register Indirect Mode:</b> Register R contains memory address: <b>EA = [R]</b>. (1 memory reference).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;6. <b>PC-Relative Mode:</b> Used in branch jumps: <b>EA = PC + Offset</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;7. <b>Indexed Mode:</b> Used in array traversal: <b>EA = Base Address + [Index Register]</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In PC-Relative mode, PC is already incremented to point to the NEXT instruction before computing EA = PC + Offset!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            9.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Instruction Formats & Opcode Expansion\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Instruction Word Fields:</b> [ Opcode | Mode | Register Address | Memory Address ].<br/>• <b>Number of Addresses:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 3-Address: ADD R1, R2, R3 (R1 = R2 + R3).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 2-Address: ADD R1, R2 (R1 = R1 + R2).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 1-Address: ADD X (Accumulator AC = AC + M[X]).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 0-Address: ADD (Stack machine: pops top two operands, adds, pushes result).<br/>• <b>Expanding Opcode Technique:</b> Allows variable length opcodes to support many instructions with different address counts in a fixed word size.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In an expanding opcode question, unused bit patterns from k-bit opcodes are multiplied by 2^(extra bits) to calculate how many shorter-address instructions can be created.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            9.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            RISC vs CISC Architecture Differences\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Simple, single-cycle instructions with fixed instruction length.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Load/Store Architecture:</b> Arithmetic operations only occur between registers.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Hardwired control unit, heavily pipelined, large register file.<br/>• <b>CISC (Complex Instruction Set Computer):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Complex, multi-cycle instructions with variable instruction length.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Memory-to-memory operations allowed (e.g. ADD directly to memory).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Microprogrammed control unit with fewer general registers.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In RISC processors, ALU instructions CANNOT access memory directly! Data must be explicitly loaded into registers using LOAD/STORE instructions.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Memory References for Different Modes\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> An instruction is stored at memory location 1000. It has opcode, mode, and address field 500. Memory at 500 contains 800, and memory at 800 contains 25. How many memory references are required to fetch and execute the instruction in Indirect Addressing Mode?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Instruction fetch requires 1 memory reference (read address 1000).<br/>• Step 2: Read address field 500 from memory to find effective address pointer: reads 800 (1 memory reference).<br/>• Step 3: Read effective address 800 from memory to fetch operand 25 (1 memory reference).<br/>• Step 4: Total memory references = 1 (fetch) + 2 (operand fetch) = 3 references.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total memory references = 3 (1 for instruction fetch + 2 for operand fetch).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Do not forget the 1 memory reference needed to FETCH the instruction itself!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Immediate addressing requires 1 memory reference for instruction fetch, but 0 references for operand fetch.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Treasure Map Coordinates\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Immediate Addressing: Cash in your pocket ($20 bill right now).\n• Direct Addressing: Mailing a letter to a specific house address (e.g. 104 Main St).\n• Indirect Addressing: A bank locker key inside a drawer. You open the drawer (first address) to find the key that opens the real locker (second address)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            10.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Booth\'s Multiplication Rules (Q_i, Q_{i-1})\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Booth\'s Algorithm multiplies signed 2\'s complement numbers efficiently.<br/>• <b>Look at two bits (Q_0, Q_{-1}):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>00:</b> Do nothing (only arithmetic shift right A, Q, Q_{-1}).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>01:</b> End of a string of 1s ⇒ <b>Add Multiplicand (A = A + M)</b>, then shift right.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>10:</b> Beginning of a string of 1s ⇒ <b>Subtract Multiplicand (A = A - M)</b>, then shift right.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>11:</b> Do nothing (only arithmetic shift right).<br/>• <b>Best Case:</b> Alternating 0s or all 1s/all 0s (minimum additions/subtractions).<br/>• <b>Worst Case:</b> Isolated 1s (e.g. 01010101): requires n additions/subtractions.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Booth\'s algorithm, the shift operation is always an ARITHMETIC SHIFT RIGHT (ASR), which preserves the sign bit (MSB is replicated)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            10.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Hardwired vs Microprogrammed Control Unit\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Hardwired Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Implemented using combinational logic gates, decoders, and flip-flops.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Extremely fast execution</b>, but inflexible (cannot modify instructions without redesigning hardware). Used in RISC.<br/>• <b>Microprogrammed Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Control signals generated by reading micro-instructions from Control Memory (ROM).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Flexible and easy to update, but slower due to memory access time. Used in CISC.<br/>• <b>Horizontal vs Vertical Microprogramming:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Horizontal:</b> 1 bit per control signal (no decoding, very wide, very fast).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Vertical:</b> Control signals encoded into groups (narrower, requires decoders, slower).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If a control unit has k mutually exclusive control signals, Horizontal needs k bits, whereas Vertical needs only ⌈log2(k + 1)⌉ bits!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            10.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            DMA (Direct Memory Access) & Cycle Stealing\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>What is DMA?</b> Allows high-speed I/O devices (disk, network) to transfer blocks of data directly to/from memory without CPU intervention.<br/>• <b>Transfer Modes:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Burst Mode:</b> DMA holds bus until entire block is transferred. CPU is paused.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Cycle Stealing Mode:</b> DMA transfers 1 word per bus cycle, stealing cycles when CPU does not need system bus.<br/>• <b>CPU Paused Percentage in Cycle Stealing:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>% CPU Slowdown = (DMA Time per second / Total Time) × 100%</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;= (Device Data Rate / Bus Bandwidth) × 100%.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Cycle Stealing: Percentage of CPU time consumed = (Data preparation time per byte / Bus cycle time)^(-1) = (Device transfer rate / Bus bandwidth) × 100%.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: DMA Cycle Stealing Percentage\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A hard disk transfers data at 2 MB/sec. The system bus has a transfer speed of 10 MB/sec. What percentage of CPU cycle time is stolen by the DMA controller during transfer?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Identify Device transfer rate = 2 MB/sec.<br/>• Step 2: Identify Bus bandwidth = 10 MB/sec.<br/>• Step 3: Formula: CPU Time Stolen = (Device Rate / Bus Rate) × 100%.<br/>• Step 4: Compute: (2 MB/s / 10 MB/s) × 100% = 0.2 × 100% = 20%.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ CPU cycle time stolen = 20%.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Do not confuse horizontal microprogramming (unencoded, 1 bit per signal) with vertical (encoded into log2 bits)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In DMA questions, verify units: bytes/sec vs words/sec and bits vs bytes.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Speed Multiplication Trick & Direct Flight DMA\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Booth\'s Algorithm: Like converting 99 × 7 into (100 - 1) × 7 = 700 - 7 = 693. Instead of adding 99 times, you subtract once and add once!\n• DMA (Direct Memory Access): Like a freight train delivering cargo straight to the warehouse (RAM) without asking the airport CEO (CPU) to inspect each individual parcel.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            11.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Cache Address Splitting in Mapping Techniques\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • A main memory physical address is partitioned into 3 fields:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Direct Mapped Cache:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- [ Tag Bits | Line / Block Bits | Word / Byte Offset ]<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Word Offset = <b>log2(Block Size in bytes)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Line Bits = <b>log2(Number of Cache Lines)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Tag Bits = Address Bits - Line Bits - Word Offset.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>k-Way Set-Associative Cache:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- [ Tag Bits | Set Bits | Word / Byte Offset ]<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Number of Sets = <b>(Total Cache Lines) / k</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Set Bits = <b>log2(Number of Sets)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Fully Associative Cache:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- [ Tag Bits | Word / Byte Offset ] (Zero set/line bits! A block can go anywhere).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In k-way set-associative cache: Number of Sets = Total Cache Size / (k × Block Size). Tag Directory Size = (Number of Lines) × (Tag Bits + Valid Bit + Dirty Bit)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            11.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Average Memory Access Time (AMAT)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Hierarchical Organization (CPU checks L1 first; on miss, visits L2/Memory):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AMAT = t_{hit_L1} + (Miss_Rate_L1 × Miss_Penalty_L1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If L2 is present: Miss_Penalty_L1 = t_{hit_L2} + (Miss_Rate_L2 × t_{memory}).<br/>• <b>Simultaneous Access (CPU searches L1 and Main Memory in parallel):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AMAT = (Hit_Rate × t_{cache}) + (Miss_Rate × t_{memory})</b>.<br/>• <b>Write Policies:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Write-Through:</b> Updates both cache and memory immediately (slow, needs write buffer).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Write-Back:</b> Updates only cache; updates memory only when dirty block is evicted (faster, needs Dirty Bit).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Unless the question explicitly specifies \'simultaneous access\', ALWAYS use the Hierarchical formula where Miss Penalty includes access to the lower level!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            11.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            The 3 Cs of Cache Misses\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>1. Compulsory (Cold) Miss:</b> First-ever access to a memory block. Unavoidable unless prefetching is used.<br/>• <b>2. Capacity Miss:</b> Cache is completely full and cannot hold all working-set blocks. Solved by increasing cache size.<br/>• <b>3. Conflict Miss:</b> Multiple blocks map to the same set/line even though other sets are empty. Occurs in Direct-Mapped and Set-Associative caches.<br/>• <b>Zero Conflict Misses:</b> Fully Associative cache has ZERO conflict misses!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fully Associative caches eliminate Conflict misses completely! They only suffer from Compulsory and Capacity misses.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Calculating Tag, Set, and Offset Bits\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A 32-bit byte-addressable system has a 64 KB 4-way set-associative cache with 32-byte blocks. Find the number of Tag, Set, and Word Offset bits.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Word Offset = log2(Block Size) = log2(32 bytes) = 5 bits.<br/>• Step 2: Total number of cache lines = Cache Size / Block Size = 64 KB / 32 B = 65536 / 32 = 2048 lines.<br/>• Step 3: Number of sets = Total Lines / k = 2048 / 4 = 512 sets.<br/>• Step 4: Set bits = log2(512) = 9 bits.<br/>• Step 5: Tag bits = 32 - (Set bits + Offset bits) = 32 - (9 + 5) = 32 - 14 = 18 bits.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Tag = 18 bits, Set = 9 bits, Word Offset = 5 bits.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Watch out for byte-addressable vs word-addressable systems! If word size is 4 bytes, offset uses word count instead of byte count.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Total cache size is data size only; tag directory overhead is NOT counted unless specifically requested.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Desk, Bookshelf & City Library\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Memory Hierarchy: CPU Registers are the textbook open on your desk right now (instant access). Cache is the shelf within arm\'s reach (few seconds). RAM is the university library across the street (minutes). Hard disk is ordering a book from another country (days)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('COA', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            12.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            The Three Classic Pipeline Hazards\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>1. Structural Hazard (Resource Conflict):</b> Hardware cannot support all concurrent instructions (e.g. single memory port accessed by IF and MEM simultaneously). Solved by separate Instruction and Data caches (Harvard Architecture).<br/>• <b>2. Data Hazard (Dependencies between instructions):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>RAW (Read After Write - True Dependency):</b> Instruction tries to read a register before prior instruction writes to it. Solved by <b>Operand Forwarding (Bypassing)</b> or compiler stalls.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>WAR (Write After Read - Anti-dependency):</b> Cannot happen in classic in-order RISC pipeline.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>WAW (Write After Write - Output dependency):</b> Cannot happen in in-order pipelines.<br/>• <b>3. Control Hazard (Branch Instruction):</b> Pipeline fetches wrong instructions before branch condition and target address are resolved. Solved by Branch Prediction, Delayed Branching, or target buffers.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a standard 5-stage in-order RISC pipeline (IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB), only RAW hazards cause data stalls! WAR and WAW hazards only occur in out-of-order execution processors.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            12.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Pipeline Clock Period, Speedup & CPI\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Pipeline Clock Cycle (τ):</b> Set by the slowest stage plus register delay:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>τ = max(stage delays) + d</b>.<br/>• <b>Time to execute n instructions in a k-stage pipeline:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>T_pipe = [ k + (n - 1) + Stalls ] × τ</b>.<br/>• <b>Speedup (S) of Pipeline over Non-pipelined:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>S = T_{non-pipe} / T_{pipe} = (n × k × τ) / [ (k + n - 1) × τ ]</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;For large n (n → ∞) and ideal CPI = 1: <b>Maximum Speedup = k</b> (number of stages!).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Ideal CPI of an un-stalled pipeline is 1. If branch stalls or data hazards occur: Effective CPI = 1 + (Stall cycles per instruction). Actual Speedup = k / Effective CPI!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Pipeline Speedup with Hazards\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A 5-stage pipeline has stage delays of 150 ps, 120 ps, 180 ps, 160 ps, 140 ps with a pipeline register delay of 20 ps. In a non-pipelined implementation, total execution time per instruction is 750 ps. Calculate the clock period and maximum speedup for a large number of instructions.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Clock period τ = max(stage delays) + register delay = 180 ps + 20 ps = 200 ps.<br/>• Step 2: Non-pipelined time per instruction = 750 ps.<br/>• Step 3: Ideal pipelined time per instruction (n → ∞) = 1 instruction per clock cycle = 200 ps.<br/>• Step 4: Speedup S = 750 ps / 200 ps = 3.75.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Clock period = 200 ps; Speedup = 3.75.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Do not forget to add the pipeline register delay to the maximum stage delay!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Speedup is k only when all stages are perfectly balanced and register delay is zero.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Laundromat Assembly Line\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Laundry Pipelining: Person 1 washes, Person 2 dries, Person 3 folds, Person 4 stores. Instead of waiting 2 hours per load, a new load completes every 30 minutes! Pipelining increases throughput (instructions per second), NOT the latency of a single instruction.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('PDS', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            13.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Pointers & Array Equivalence in C\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>What is an array name?</b> In C, an array name <code>arr</code> is a pointer to its first element: <code>arr == &arr[0]</code>.<br/>• <b>Subscript Rule:</b> <code>arr[i] == *(arr + i) == *(i + arr) == i[arr]</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- In C, <code>3[arr]</code> is completely valid and equals <code>arr[3]</code>!<br/>• <b>Pointer Increment Nuances:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>*p++</code>: Read <code>*p</code> first, then increment pointer <code>p</code> to next memory slot.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>(*p)++</code>: Read value, increment the integer value inside memory by 1; pointer stays at same spot.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>*++p</code>: Increment pointer <code>p</code> first, then dereference.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: When an array is passed to a function, it decays to a simple pointer! Inside the function, sizeof(arr) gives the size of a pointer (4 or 8 bytes), NOT the full array size!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            13.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Short-Circuit Evaluation in C\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Logical AND (expr1 && expr2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If expr1 evaluates to <b>0 (False)</b>, expr2 is <b>NEVER EXECUTED</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any side-effects (like <code>x++</code> or function calls) inside expr2 are completely skipped.<br/>• <b>Logical OR (expr1 || expr2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If expr1 evaluates to <b>non-zero (True)</b>, expr2 is <b>NEVER EXECUTED</b>!<br/>• <b>Precedence:</b> Logical AND (&&) has higher precedence than Logical OR (||).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In expression (a++ || b++): if a starts non-zero (True), b++ is NEVER evaluated, and b remains completely unchanged!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            13.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Storage Classes & Scope in C\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>auto:</b> Default for local variables. Stored in Stack; scope is block-local; lifetime is function call.<br/>• <b>register:</b> Request to store in CPU register for speed. Cannot take its address with <code>&var</code>!<br/>• <b>static:</b> Stored in Data Segment (BSS/Data). Retains its value across multiple function calls; initialized only once to 0.<br/>• <b>extern:</b> Declares a global variable defined in another file/scope without allocating new memory.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Static local variables are initialized ONLY ONCE when the program starts. On subsequent calls to the function, the initialization line is completely ignored!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Tracing Pointer Expressions in C\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given int a[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}; int *p = a; What is the output of printf(\'%d %d\', *p++, (*p)++); followed by printf(\'%d\', *p); ?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Pointer p points to a[0] (value 10).<br/>• Step 2: Note that in C, argument evaluation order in printf is unspecified, but let\'s trace step by step sequentially:<br/>• Step 3: *p++ fetches 10, then advances p to point to a[1] (20).<br/>• Step 4: (*p)++ increments the value at a[1] from 20 to 21, but expression evaluates to 20.<br/>• Step 5: Afterwards, a[1] is now 21, and *p points to a[1] with value 21.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ a[1] becomes 21; value pointed to by p is 21.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          sizeof(\'a\') in C is sizeof(int) = 4 bytes! In C++, sizeof(\'a\') is 1 byte.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Array out-of-bounds access in C produces Undefined Behavior, not a compilation error or runtime crash!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Russian Matryoshka Doll (Recursion)\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Recursion: Opening a Russian nesting doll to find a smaller doll inside. You keep unpacking until you hit the smallest solid doll (Base Case). Then you pack them all back together (Call Stack unwinding)!\n• Array Decay: Handing someone a business card with your home address instead of shipping your entire house to them.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('PDS', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            14.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Infix, Postfix & Stack Conversions\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Postfix Evaluation using Stack:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Scan expression from left to right.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If operand (number), push onto stack.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If operator: pop two operands (op2 = pop(), op1 = pop()).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Compute: <b>result = op1 (operator) op2</b>. Push result back onto stack.<br/>• <b>Stack Permutations:</b> Number of valid stack permutations of n elements = <b>Catalan Number C_n = (1 / (n + 1)) × C(2n, n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Values: C_1 = 1, C_2 = 2, C_3 = 5, C_4 = 14, C_5 = 42.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In postfix evaluation, when an operator arrives: the first popped item is the RIGHT operand (op2), and the second popped item is the LEFT operand (op1): result = op1 op op2!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            14.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Circular Queue Boundary Conditions\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Using an array of size <b>N</b> with front and rear pointers:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Enqueue:</b> <code>rear = (rear + 1) % N; queue[rear] = item;</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dequeue:</b> <code>front = (front + 1) % N; item = queue[front];</code><br/>• <b>Full Condition (leaving 1 empty slot):</b> <b>(rear + 1) % N == front</b>.<br/>• <b>Empty Condition:</b> <b>front == rear</b>.<br/>• <b>Number of elements currently in Queue:</b> <b>(rear - front + N) % N</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a circular queue of array size N, leaving one empty slot allows max capacity of (N - 1) elements to distinguish Full from Empty!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            14.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Linked Lists & Floyd\'s Cycle Detection\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Floyd\'s Tortoise and Hare Algorithm:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Slow pointer moves 1 step; Fast pointer moves 2 steps.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If they meet, a <b>cycle exists</b> in the linked list!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Finding Start of Cycle:</b> Reset slow to Head. Keep fast at meeting point. Move both 1 step at a time. The node where they meet is the exact cycle entry node!<br/>• <b>Reversing a Singly Linked List:</b> Done in O(N) time and O(1) auxiliary space using 3 pointers (prev, curr, next).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Floyd\'s cycle detection runs in O(N) time with O(1) extra space! The distance from Head to cycle-start equals distance from meeting-point to cycle-start.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Evaluating Postfix Expression\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Evaluate the postfix expression: 6 2 3 + * 3 8 2 / + * 2 ^ 3 +\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Push 6, push 2, push 3. Stack: [6, 2, 3].<br/>• Step 2: \'+\' arrives: pop 3 and 2. Compute 2 + 3 = 5. Push 5. Stack: [6, 5].<br/>• Step 3: \'*\' arrives: pop 5 and 6. Compute 6 * 5 = 30. Push 30. Stack: [30].<br/>• Step 4: Push 3, push 8, push 2. Stack: [30, 3, 8, 2].<br/>• Step 5: \'/\' arrives: pop 2 and 8. Compute 8 / 2 = 4. Push 4. Stack: [30, 3, 4].<br/>• Step 6: \'+\' arrives: pop 4 and 3. Compute 3 + 4 = 7. Push 7. Stack: [30, 7].<br/>• Step 7: \'*\' arrives: pop 7 and 30. Compute 30 * 7 = 210. Push 210. Stack: [210].\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Evaluated result = 210 (before remaining operations).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In postfix to infix, parentheses must be preserved when operator precedence demands it!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Double-ended queue (Deque) can implement both a Stack and a Queue in O(1) time per operation.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Browser Back Button & Cafeteria Line\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Stack (LIFO): The \'Back\' button in your web browser. The last page you visited is the first page that pops back up.\n• Queue (FIFO): Standing in line at a cinema ticket counter. First person in line gets the ticket first.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('PDS', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            15.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Binary Tree Fundamental Properties\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Key Formulas for Binary Trees:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Leaf vs Degree-2 Nodes:</b> In any binary tree: <b>n_0 = n_2 + 1</b> (Number of leaf nodes = Number of degree-2 nodes + 1).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Max nodes at height h (root at h=0):</b> <b>2^(h + 1) - 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Min height for n nodes:</b> <b>⌈log2(n + 1)⌉ - 1</b>.<br/>• <b>Unique Tree Reconstruction:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Inorder + Preorder gives a UNIQUE binary tree.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Inorder + Postorder gives a UNIQUE binary tree.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Preorder + Postorder does NOT give a unique tree (unless strictly full binary tree)!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Inorder traversal MUST be given to uniquely reconstruct a binary tree! Preorder + Postorder is NEVER sufficient for general binary trees.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            15.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Binary Search Trees (BST) & AVL Trees\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>BST Inorder Traversal:</b> Inorder traversal of any BST always produces strictly ASCENDING sorted order.<br/>• <b>Number of distinct BSTs with n distinct keys:</b> <b>Catalan Number C_n</b>.<br/>• <b>AVL Trees (Height-Balanced BST):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Balance Factor = <b>Height(Left Subtree) - Height(Right Subtree) ∈ {-1, 0, +1}</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 4 Rebalancing Rotations: LL, RR (single rotations) and LR, RL (double rotations).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Minimum nodes in AVL of height h: <b>N(h) = N(h - 1) + N(h - 2) + 1</b> (with N(0)=1, N(1)=2).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In any BST, the node with maximum value has NO right child, and the node with minimum value has NO left child!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            15.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Binary Heap Array Representation & Complexities\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • In a 1-based index array representation for a node at index <b>i</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Parent index: <b>⌊i / 2⌋</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Left child index: <b>2i</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Right child index: <b>2i + 1</b>.<br/>• <b>Heap Operations Time Complexity:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Find Min/Max: <b>O(1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Insert: <b>O(log n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Delete Min/Max: <b>O(log n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Build-Heap (from arbitrary array):</b> <b>O(n)</b> time (NOT O(n log n)! Tested repeatedly in GATE!).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Building a heap of n elements takes O(n) time, NOT O(n log n)! Sum of heights ∑ (h / 2^h) converges to 2, proving linear time.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Reconstructing Tree from Preorder & Inorder\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given Preorder: [A, B, D, E, C, F] and Inorder: [D, B, E, A, F, C]. Find the Postorder traversal.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: First element in Preorder is Root = A.<br/>• Step 2: Locate A in Inorder: Left subtree is {D, B, E}, Right subtree is {F, C}.<br/>• Step 3: In Preorder, next 3 elements {B, D, E} belong to Left subtree. Root of Left subtree is B.<br/>• Step 4: Locate B in Inorder: D is Left child of B, E is Right child of B.<br/>• Step 5: For Right subtree: Preorder has C first ⇒ C is root; Inorder has F before C ⇒ F is Left child of C.<br/>• Step 6: Compute Postorder (Left, Right, Root): D, E, B, F, C, A.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Postorder: D, E, B, F, C, A.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In a Min-Heap, the maximum element is always at one of the leaf nodes (indices ⌊n/2⌋ + 1 to n)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Deleting an arbitrary node from a heap takes O(log n) time, provided its index is known.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Corporate Hierarchy & Hospital Emergency Triage\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Binary Tree: Like a CEO who has at most 2 direct Vice Presidents, each VP having at most 2 directors.\n• Heap (Priority Queue): Like an emergency room triage where patients are treated by severity of injury (highest priority first), regardless of arrival time!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            16.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Big-O, Big-Omega & Big-Theta Explained\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Big-O (O):</b> Upper Bound: f(n) ≤ c · g(n) for all n ≥ n_0.<br/>• <b>Big-Omega (Ω):</b> Lower Bound: f(n) ≥ c · g(n) for all n ≥ n_0.<br/>• <b>Big-Theta (Θ):</b> Tight Bound: c1·g(n) ≤ f(n) ≤ c2·g(n). True if and only if both O and Ω hold!<br/>• <b>Standard Rate of Growth Hierarchy:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>c &lt; log(log n) &lt; log n &lt; √n &lt; n &lt; n log n &lt; n² &lt; n³ &lt; 2^n &lt; n! &lt; n^n</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: f(n) = Θ(g(n)) IF AND ONLY IF f(n) = O(g(n)) AND f(n) = Ω(g(n)). If one fails, Θ cannot hold!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            16.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Master Theorem for Divide and Conquer\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • For recurrence: <b>T(n) = a · T(n / b) + Θ(n^k · log^p n)</b> (with a ≥ 1, b > 1):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Compare <b>a</b> with <b>b^k</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Case 1 (a > b^k):</b> <b>T(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a))</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Case 2 (a = b^k):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• If p > -1: <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^{p+1} n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• If p = -1: T(n) = Θ(n^k · log(log n)).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Case 3 (a < b^k):</b> <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^p n)</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Master theorem CANNOT be applied if: (1) a is not constant (e.g. 2^n T(n/2)), (2) b is not constant, or (3) f(n) is not a polynomial (e.g. T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n!).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Solving Recurrence with Master Theorem\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Solve the recurrence relation T(n) = 4 T(n / 2) + n².\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Identify parameters: a = 4, b = 2, f(n) = n² ⇒ k = 2, p = 0.<br/>• Step 2: Calculate b^k = 2² = 4.<br/>• Step 3: Compare a with b^k: a = 4 and b^k = 4 ⇒ a == b^k (Case 2).<br/>• Step 4: Since p = 0 > -1, formula gives T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^{p+1} n) = Θ(n² · log n).\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ T(n) = Θ(n² log n).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In T(n) = 2 T(n/2) + n/log n: Master Theorem fails because f(n) is not polynomially larger/smaller!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Master theorem for subtracting recurrences: T(n) = T(n - 1) + n gives Θ(n²).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Speed of Transport Modes\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Big-O: An upper bound promise. Like saying \'a flight from Delhi to Mumbai takes at most 3 hours\'. It might take 2 hours, but will never exceed 3 hours in the worst case.\n• Master Theorem: A standard cookbook recipe for dividing a giant task into smaller sub-tasks.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            17.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Master Summary of Sorting Algorithms\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Comparison Table for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Insertion Sort:</b> Best: O(n), Avg: O(n²), Worst: O(n²), Space: O(1), <b>Stable</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Merge Sort:</b> Best: O(n log n), Avg: O(n log n), Worst: O(n log n), Space: O(n), <b>Stable</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Heap Sort:</b> Best: O(n log n), Avg: O(n log n), Worst: O(n log n), Space: O(1), <b>NOT Stable</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Quick Sort:</b> Best: O(n log n), Avg: O(n log n), Worst: O(n²), Space: O(log n), <b>NOT Stable</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Counting Sort:</b> O(n + k) time and O(k) space (non-comparison, Stable).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Merge Sort and Insertion Sort are STABLE! Quick Sort and Heap Sort are UNSTABLE! The theoretical lower bound for any comparison-based sort is Ω(n log n).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            17.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Hash Table Collisions: Chaining vs Open Addressing\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Separate Chaining:</b> Each slot points to a linked list. Load factor α can exceed 1.<br/>• <b>Open Addressing:</b> All elements stored inside the hash table array. Load factor α ≤ 1.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Linear Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h\'(k) + i) % m</code>. Suffers from <b>Primary Clustering</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Quadratic Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h\'(k) + c1·i + c2·i²) % m</code>. Suffers from <b>Secondary Clustering</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Double Hashing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h1(k) + i · h2(k)) % m</code>. Eliminates clustering (h2(k) must be coprime to m).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In open addressing, successful search takes O((1/α) ln(1/(1-α))) and unsuccessful search takes O(1 / (1 - α)) expected probes!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Linear Probing Hash Collisions\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Insert keys [12, 22, 32, 42] into a hash table of size 10 using hash function h(k) = k % 10 with Linear Probing.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: 12 % 10 = 2 ⇒ Slot 2 is empty. Store 12 at index 2.<br/>• Step 2: 22 % 10 = 2 ⇒ Collision! Probe (2 + 1) % 10 = 3. Slot 3 is empty. Store 22 at index 3.<br/>• Step 3: 32 % 10 = 2 ⇒ Collision at 2, collision at 3! Probe (2 + 2) % 10 = 4. Slot 4 is empty. Store 32 at index 4.<br/>• Step 4: 42 % 10 = 2 ⇒ Collisions at 2, 3, 4! Probe (2 + 3) % 10 = 5. Slot 5 is empty. Store 42 at index 5.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Final positions: 12 at 2, 22 at 3, 32 at 4, 42 at 5.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In QuickSort, picking the first or last element as pivot on an ALREADY SORTED array results in WORST-CASE O(n²) time!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Randomized QuickSort avoids worst-case for sorted inputs with high probability, but theoretical worst case is still O(n²).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Organizing Playing Cards & Coat Check Tickets\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Insertion Sort: Like sorting playing cards in your hand—you pick each new card and slide it into its correct position among already sorted cards.\n• Hash Table: Like a coat checkroom with 100 hooks. You get a numbered ticket that instantly maps to your exact hook without searching the whole room.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            18.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Greedy Method vs Dynamic Programming\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Greedy Method:</b> Makes locally optimal choice at each step hoping for global optimum. Never backtracks.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: Fractional Knapsack, Huffman Coding, Kruskal\'s, Prim\'s, Dijkstra.<br/>• <b>Dynamic Programming (DP):</b> Solves subproblems once, stores results in a table (memoization/tabulation).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Requires <b>Optimal Substructure</b> AND <b>Overlapping Subproblems</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: 0/1 Knapsack, Longest Common Subsequence (LCS), Matrix Chain Multiplication, Floyd-Warshall.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fractional Knapsack is solved by Greedy (sort by value/weight in O(n log n)). 0/1 Knapsack CANNOT be solved by Greedy; it requires Dynamic Programming in O(n × W) pseudo-polynomial time!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            18.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) Recurrence\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • For strings X of length m and Y of length n:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <b>X[i] == Y[j]</b>: <b>LCS[i, j] = 1 + LCS[i - 1, j - 1]</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <b>X[i] ≠ Y[j]</b>: <b>LCS[i, j] = max(LCS[i - 1, j], LCS[i, j - 1])</b>.<br/>• <b>Time & Space:</b> O(m × n) time, reducible to O(min(m, n)) space using two rows.<br/>• <b>Matrix Chain Multiplication:</b> Cost recurrence: <b>m[i, j] = min_{i ≤ k < j} { m[i, k] + m[k+1, j] + p_{i-1}·p_k·p_j }</b>. Time: O(n³).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Matrix Chain Multiplication with n matrices, number of different parenthesizations is given by Catalan Number C_{n-1}!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Huffman Code Tree Construction\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given frequencies: a: 5, b: 9, c: 12, d: 13, e: 16, f: 45. Find the length of Huffman encoded string.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Pick two smallest: 5 and 9 ⇒ merge to node 14. Remaining: [c:12, d:13, (5+9):14, e:16, f:45].<br/>• Step 2: Pick two smallest: 12 and 13 ⇒ merge to node 25. Remaining: [14, e:16, 25, f:45].<br/>• Step 3: Pick two smallest: 14 and 16 ⇒ merge to node 30. Remaining: [25, 30, f:45].<br/>• Step 4: Pick two smallest: 25 and 30 ⇒ merge to node 55. Remaining: [f:45, 55].<br/>• Step 5: Merge 45 and 55 ⇒ root 100.<br/>• Step 6: Code lengths: f=1 bit, c=3, d=3, e=3, a=4, b=4. Weighted path length = (45×1) + (12×3) + (13×3) + (16×3) + (5×4) + (9×4) = 45 + 36 + 39 + 48 + 20 + 36 = 224 bits.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total encoded length = 224 bits.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Huffman code is a prefix code: NO valid codeword is a prefix of any other codeword!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Huffman tree is always a strictly Full Binary Tree (every internal node has exactly 2 children).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Cashier Change & Remembering Past Math Results\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Greedy: Like a cashier giving you change using the fewest coins by always picking the largest coin denomination first (25c, then 10c, etc.).\n• Dynamic Programming: \'1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4\'. If I write another \'+ 1\', you instantly say \'5\' because you REMEMBERED the previous 4 without recalculating from scratch!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('ALGO', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            19.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            BFS vs DFS Comparison & Applications\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Breadth-First Search (BFS):</b> Uses a <b>Queue</b>. Time: <b>O(V + E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Finds the <b>shortest path in unweighted graphs</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects bipartiteness (2-coloring).<br/>• <b>Depth-First Search (DFS):</b> Uses a <b>Stack / Recursion</b>. Time: <b>O(V + E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects cycles in directed graphs (via <b>Back Edges</b>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Computes <b>Topological Sort</b> of a DAG (reverse of finish times).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Finds Strongly Connected Components (Kosaraju\'s / Tarjan\'s algorithm).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In DFS of a directed graph, a cycle exists IF AND ONLY IF there is at least one BACK EDGE! Forward and Cross edges do NOT indicate a cycle.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            19.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Dijkstra vs Bellman-Ford vs Floyd-Warshall\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Dijkstra\'s Algorithm:</b> Single-Source Shortest Path for <b>non-negative edge weights</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Time: <b>O((V + E) log V)</b> using min-heap. FAILS for negative edge weights!<br/>• <b>Bellman-Ford Algorithm:</b> Works with <b>negative edge weights</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Relaxes all edges (V - 1) times. Time: <b>O(V × E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects negative-weight cycles (if distance decreases on V-th relaxation).<br/>• <b>Floyd-Warshall Algorithm:</b> All-Pairs Shortest Path in <b>O(V³)</b> time using dynamic programming.<br/>• <b>Kruskal vs Prim (MST):</b> Kruskal uses edges (O(E log E) with DSU); Prim grows from a vertex (O(E + V log V)).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If all edge weights in a graph are distinct, the Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) is 100% UNIQUE! Shortest path tree is NOT necessarily unique.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Kruskal\'s MST Edge Selection\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given edges sorted by weight: (A-B, 1), (B-C, 2), (A-C, 2), (C-D, 3), (B-D, 4). Which edges are included in the MST?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Pick (A-B, 1) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.<br/>• Step 2: Pick (B-C, 2) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.<br/>• Step 3: Consider (A-C, 2) ⇒ A, B, C are already connected! Adding A-C creates cycle A-B-C-A. REJECT.<br/>• Step 4: Pick (C-D, 3) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.<br/>• Step 5: We have 4 vertices and 3 edges (V - 1 = 3). MST is complete!\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ MST edges: (A-B, 1), (B-C, 2), (C-D, 3). Total weight = 6.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Dijkstra\'s algorithm can enter an infinite loop or give wrong answers if negative edge weights are present!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Topological sort exists IF AND ONLY IF the graph is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: GPS Navigation & Google Maps\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Dijkstra\'s Algorithm: How Google Maps finds the quickest driving route to your destination without negative road tolls.\n• Minimum Spanning Tree: Laying down fiber-optic internet cables between cities with the absolute minimum total cable length while ensuring every city is connected.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('TOC', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            20.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            DFA vs NFA Equivalences & Power\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Equivalent Power:</b> Every NFA can be converted into an equivalent DFA using <b>Subset Construction</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If an NFA has <b>n states</b>, the equivalent minimized DFA has at most <b>2^n states</b>.<br/>• <b>Key Distinctions:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. In DFA: For every state and input symbol, there is <b>exactly ONE</b> transition: δ: Q × Σ → Q.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. In NFA: There can be 0, 1, or multiple transitions, plus ε-transitions: δ: Q × (Σ ∪ {ε}) → 2^Q.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Dead States:</b> DFA requires explicit trap/dead states for invalid inputs; NFA simply drops the transition.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: DFA, NFA, and ε-NFA all have EQUAL computational power! They all recognize the exact same family of languages: Regular Languages.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            20.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Regular Expressions & Arden\'s Theorem\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Arden\'s Theorem:</b> If P and Q are regular expressions over Σ and P does not contain ε, then equation:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>R = Q + RP</b> has a <b>UNIQUE solution: R = QP*</b>.<br/>• <b>Identities to Memorize for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• (r*)* = r*<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• (r + s)* = (r* · s*)* = (r* + s*)*<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• r(s · r)* = (r · s)* · r<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• ε + r · r* = r* = ε + r* · r\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Arden\'s theorem R = Q + RP gives the unique solution R = QP*. Notice P is on the right of R! If R = Q + PR (left recursion), the solution is P*Q.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            20.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Regular Language Closure Properties\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Regular Languages are CLOSED under:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Union:</b> L1 ∪ L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Intersection:</b> L1 ∩ L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Complement:</b> L\' is Regular (swap accept and non-accept states in DFA!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Difference:</b> L1 - L2 = L1 ∩ L2\' is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Concatenation:</b> L1 · L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Kleene Star:</b> L* is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Reversal:</b> L^R is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Homomorphism & Inverse Homomorphism:</b> Regular.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Regular languages are closed under ALL standard boolean and algebraic operations! To complement a DFA, swap final and non-final states (make sure it is a complete DFA first, including trap states)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Minimum DFA States for String Ending in \'01\'\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Find the minimum number of states in a DFA accepting all binary strings ending with substring \'01\'.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Length of pattern \'01\' is 2.<br/>• Step 2: For strings ending with a specific pattern of length k, minimum DFA states = k + 1.<br/>• Step 3: Here k = 2 ⇒ States = 2 + 1 = 3 states: q0 (start/empty), q1 (last bit 0), q2 (last bits 01 - Accept).<br/>• Step 4: Trace transitions: from q2 on \'0\' go to q1; on \'1\' go to q0.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Minimum states = 3.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Swapping final and non-final states on an NFA does NOT give the complement language! You must convert NFA to DFA first.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Pumping Lemma can prove a language is NOT regular; it can NEVER prove a language IS regular.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Vending Machine States & Traffic Light Controller\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Finite Automata: Like a soda vending machine. Inserting a coin moves it from state \'Waiting\' to \'Paid\'. Pressing button delivers soda and returns to \'Waiting\'. The machine has finite memory—it only remembers its current state!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('TOC', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            21.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Deterministic PDA (DPDA) vs Non-Deterministic PDA (NPDA)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Crucial Distinction:</b> NPDA is <b>strictly more powerful</b> than DPDA!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- DPDA recognizes <b>Deterministic Context-Free Languages (DCFL)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- NPDA recognizes <b>Context-Free Languages (CFL)</b>.<br/>• <b>Examples to Memorize for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. L = { w c w^R | w ∈ {0,1}* } has a marker \'c\' ⇒ <b>DCFL</b> (accepted by DPDA).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. L = { w w^R | w ∈ {0,1}* } has NO marker (even palindrome) ⇒ <b>CFL, but NOT DCFL</b> (requires non-deterministic guess of midpoint!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. L = { a^n b^n c^n } ⇒ <b>NOT CFL</b> (requires 2 stacks = Turing Machine).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Unlike Finite Automata where DFA == NFA, in Pushdown Automata NPDA is STRICTLY MORE POWERFUL than DPDA: DCFL ⊂ CFL!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            21.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            CFL Closure Properties (The Famous Exceptions!)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>CFLs are CLOSED under:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Union:</b> L1 ∪ L2 is CFL.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Concatenation:</b> L1 · L2 is CFL.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Kleene Star:</b> L* is CFL.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Reversal:</b> L^R is CFL.<br/>• <b>CFLs are NOT CLOSED under:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Intersection:</b> L1 ∩ L2 is NOT necessarily CFL!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Complement:</b> L\' is NOT necessarily CFL!<br/>• <b>Intersection with Regular Language:</b> If L is CFL and R is Regular, then <b>L ∩ R is ALWAYS CFL</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: CFL is NOT closed under Intersection or Complement! But DCFL is CLOSED under Complement (though NOT closed under Union or Intersection).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            21.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            The Chomsky Hierarchy of Languages\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Type 3 (Regular):</b> Accepted by Finite Automata. Grammar: A → aB or A → a.<br/>• <b>Type 2 (Context-Free):</b> Accepted by Pushdown Automata. Grammar: A → α (single variable on LHS).<br/>• <b>Type 1 (Context-Sensitive):</b> Accepted by Linear Bounded Automata (LBA). Grammar: α → β where |α| ≤ |β|.<br/>• <b>Type 0 (Unrestricted / Recursively Enumerable):</b> Accepted by Turing Machine. Grammar: α → β.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Strict containment: Regular ⊂ DCFL ⊂ CFL ⊂ CSL ⊂ Recursive ⊂ Recursively Enumerable!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Checking CFL Intersection\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Let L1 = { a^n b^n c^m | n, m ≥ 1 } and L2 = { a^m b^n c^n | m, n ≥ 1 }. Are L1 and L2 CFLs? Is L1 ∩ L2 a CFL?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: L1 requires matching count of a\'s with b\'s (c\'s independent) ⇒ Can be done with 1 stack ⇒ L1 is CFL.<br/>• Step 2: L2 requires matching count of b\'s with c\'s (a\'s independent) ⇒ Can be done with 1 stack ⇒ L2 is CFL.<br/>• Step 3: Intersection L1 ∩ L2 = { a^n b^n c^n | n ≥ 1 }.<br/>• Step 4: L1 ∩ L2 requires comparing 3 counts simultaneously, impossible with 1 stack.<br/>• Step 5: Therefore, L1 ∩ L2 is NOT a CFL (it is a Context-Sensitive Language).\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ L1 and L2 are CFLs, but their intersection L1 ∩ L2 is NOT a CFL.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Inherent ambiguity: Some CFLs are inherently ambiguous (every grammar for them is ambiguous, e.g. {a^i b^j c^k | i=j or j=k}).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          DCFLs are NEVER ambiguous! Every DCFL has an unambiguous grammar.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Balancing Scale & Parenthesis Matcher\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • PDA Stack: Like a stack of plates in a cafeteria. To check if parentheses match: push \'(\' onto the stack; when \')\' arrives, pop \'(\' off. If stack is empty at the end, parentheses were perfectly balanced!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('TOC', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            22.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Recursive (REC) vs Recursively Enumerable (RE)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Recursive Language (REC / Decidable):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Turing Machine halts on EVERY input (Halts and Accepts on w ∈ L; Halts and Rejects on w ∉ L).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Closed under ALL standard operations</b> including Complement: If L is REC, L\' is REC.<br/>• <b>Recursively Enumerable Language (RE / Semi-Decidable):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Turing machine halts and accepts if w ∈ L.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If w ∉ L, it may reject OR run in an infinite loop forever!<br/>• <b>The Complement Duality Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>A language L is Recursive IF AND ONLY IF both L and its complement L\' are Recursively Enumerable!</b>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If L is RE and L\' is also RE, then L is 100% DECIDABLE (Recursive)! If L is RE and undecidable, then L\' CANNOT even be RE!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            22.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Rice\'s Theorem for Turing Machines\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Rice\'s Theorem (Part 1):</b> Any <b>non-trivial semantic (language) property</b> of the language accepted by a Turing Machine is <b>UNDECIDABLE</b>.<br/>• <b>What is a Semantic Property?</b> A property about the LANGUAGE recognized by the TM (e.g. \'Is L(M) empty?\', \'Is L(M) finite?\', \'Is L(M) regular?\').<br/>• <b>Non-trivial means:</b> True for some TMs and False for other TMs.<br/>• <b>Syntactic Properties (Rice does NOT apply):</b> Questions about the machine structure itself (e.g. \'Does TM have 5 states?\', \'Does TM take 10 steps on input w?\') are DECIDABLE.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In GATE, if a question asks \'Is property P decidable for a Turing Machine?\' and P is about L(M) (like regularity, emptiness, finiteness, equivalence), the answer is INSTANTLY UNDECIDABLE by Rice\'s Theorem!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Applying Rice\'s Theorem\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Determine if the following problem is decidable: \'Given a Turing Machine M, does M accept a language that contains at least one palindrome?\'\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Check if the property is semantic: \'Does L(M) contain a palindrome?\' is a property of the language recognized by M (Semantic).<br/>• Step 2: Check if non-trivial: Some TMs accept palindromes (e.g. TM for (01)*), while others accept nothing (empty language). Non-trivial!<br/>• Step 3: By Rice\'s Theorem Part 1, the problem is UNDECIDABLE.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Undecidable by Rice\'s Theorem.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          \'Does TM M take at most 100 steps on input w?\' is DECIDABLE because you can simulate it for 100 steps and stop!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Post Correspondence Problem (PCP) is undecidable for alphabet size ≥ 2, but decidable for unary alphabet (size 1).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Infinite Tape & Software Freeze\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Halting Problem: Can anyone write an antivirus software that inspects any arbitrary code and guarantees whether it will eventually terminate or freeze in an infinite loop? Alan Turing proved it is mathematically IMPOSSIBLE for all programs!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CD', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            23.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Rules for Calculating FIRST Sets\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Definition:</b> FIRST(X) is the set of terminal symbols that appear as the first symbol of any string derived from X.<br/>• <b>Three Golden Computation Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. If X is a terminal: <b>FIRST(X) = { X }</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If X → ε is a production: Add <b>ε</b> to FIRST(X).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If X → Y1 Y2 ... Yk:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Add all non-ε symbols from FIRST(Y1) to FIRST(X).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If Y1 derives ε, add all non-ε symbols from FIRST(Y2) to FIRST(X), and so forth.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If ALL Y1 ... Yk derive ε, add <b>ε</b> to FIRST(X).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Terminal symbols ALWAYS have FIRST(a) = { a }. When calculating FIRST(Y1 Y2), do not stop at Y1 if Y1 can produce ε!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            23.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Rules for Calculating FOLLOW Sets\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Definition:</b> FOLLOW(A) is the set of terminals that can appear immediately to the right of non-terminal A in some sentential form.<br/>• <b>Three Golden Computation Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. For Start Symbol S: Add <b>$ (End-of-input marker)</b> to FOLLOW(S).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. For production A → α B β:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Everything in <b>FIRST(β) except ε</b> is added to FOLLOW(B).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. For production A → α B OR A → α B β (where FIRST(β) contains ε):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Everything in <b>FOLLOW(A)</b> is added to FOLLOW(B).<br/>• <b>FOLLOW sets NEVER contain ε!</b>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: FOLLOW set NEVER CONTAINS ε (epsilon)! If your calculation produces ε in a FOLLOW set, it is 100% WRONG!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Computing FIRST and FOLLOW Sets\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given grammar: S → A B; A → a | ε; B → b | ε. Compute FIRST(S) and FOLLOW(A).\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: FIRST(A) = { a, ε }; FIRST(B) = { b, ε }.<br/>• Step 2: For S → A B: FIRST(S) includes FIRST(A) minus ε = { a }. Since A → ε, include FIRST(B) = { b }. Since both A and B can produce ε, add ε. ⇒ FIRST(S) = { a, b, ε }.<br/>• Step 3: To find FOLLOW(A): Look at S → A B. Non-terminal B immediately follows A.<br/>• Step 4: Everything in FIRST(B) except ε goes into FOLLOW(A) ⇒ { b }.<br/>• Step 5: Since B derives ε, everything in FOLLOW(S) also goes into FOLLOW(A).<br/>• Step 6: S is start symbol ⇒ $ ∈ FOLLOW(S). Since S appears nowhere on RHS, FOLLOW(S) = { $ }.<br/>• Step 7: Therefore, FOLLOW(A) = { b, $ }.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ FIRST(S) = { a, b, ε }; FOLLOW(A) = { b, $ }.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In token counting: String constants like "Hello, World!" count as ONE single token!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Identifiers vs Keywords: In C lexing, keywords like \'int\', \'return\' are reserved and not identifiers.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Spell Checker & Grammar Dictionary\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Lexer: Like checking a dictionary to see if letters form valid English words (\'int\', \'sum\', \'100\').\n• FIRST set: Like checking what letter a sentence could possibly begin with.\n• FOLLOW set: Like asking what word can legally appear immediately to the right of a noun in a sentence.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CD', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            24.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            LL(1) Grammar Conditions\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • A grammar is <b>LL(1)</b> if and only if for every non-terminal A with multiple productions A → α | β:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = ∅</b> (disjoint FIRST sets).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. At most one of α or β can derive ε.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If β derives ε, then <b>FIRST(α) ∩ FOLLOW(A) = ∅</b>.<br/>• <b>Instant Disqualifiers for LL(1):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any grammar with <b>Left Recursion (A → A α)</b> is NEVER LL(1)!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any grammar with <b>Ambiguity</b> is NEVER LL(1)!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Left recursion (immediate or indirect) INSTANTLY disqualifies a grammar from being LL(1)! Eliminate left recursion and left factoring before building LL(1) table.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            24.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Hierarchy and Power of LR Parsers\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Power Hierarchy (Strict Containment):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>LR(0) ⊂ SLR(1) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ CLR(1)</b>.<br/>• <b>Number of Parsing States:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>States in LR(0) == States in SLR(1) == States in LALR(1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- CLR(1) has significantly MORE states (splits states with different lookaheads).<br/>• <b>Shift-Reduce (SR) and Reduce-Reduce (RR) Conflicts:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- In LR(0): Reduce action placed in ALL columns.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- In SLR(1): Reduce action placed ONLY in <b>FOLLOW(A)</b> columns.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Merging states from CLR(1) to LALR(1) <b>NEVER produces Shift-Reduce conflicts</b>, but MAY produce Reduce-Reduce conflicts!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Merging CLR(1) states to form LALR(1) can NEVER introduce a new Shift-Reduce (SR) conflict! It can only introduce Reduce-Reduce (RR) conflicts.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Checking if Grammar is LL(1)\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Is grammar S → a S | a LL(1)?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Check productions for S: α = a S and β = a.<br/>• Step 2: FIRST(α) = FIRST(a S) = { a }.<br/>• Step 3: FIRST(β) = FIRST(a) = { a }.<br/>• Step 4: Check intersection: FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = { a } ∩ { a } = { a } ≠ ∅.<br/>• Step 5: Since intersection is not empty, the parser cannot decide which rule to use when lookahead is \'a\'.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Grammar is NOT LL(1) (requires left factoring to S → a S\' with S\' → S | ε).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Every SLR(1) grammar is an LALR(1) grammar, but not vice versa!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Every ambiguous grammar is neither LL(1) nor LR(k) for any k.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Predictive Text & Jigsaw Puzzle\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • LL(1) Parser: Like a predictive keyboard on your phone that looks ahead by 1 character to predict the next word without backtracking.\n• LR Parser: Like building a jigsaw puzzle from individual bottom pieces upward until the entire picture (start symbol) is assembled.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CD', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            25.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            S-Attributed vs L-Attributed SDT\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>S-Attributed Definition:</b> Uses ONLY <b>Synthesized Attributes</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Computed bottom-up in a parse tree from child values to parent: A.val = f(X1.val, ..., Xk.val).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Can be evaluated during <b>LR parsing (bottom-up)</b> without modifying parse stack.<br/>• <b>L-Attributed Definition:</b> Uses Synthesized attributes AND restricted Inherited attributes.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- An inherited attribute of node X_j can depend only on:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Attributes of symbols to its <b>LEFT (X_1, X_2, ..., X_{j-1})</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Inherited attributes of the <b>parent symbol A</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Can be evaluated during <b>Depth-First Search (left-to-right)</b> traversal.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every S-attributed SDT is automatically an L-attributed SDT! The reverse is NOT true: L-attributed includes inherited attributes that depend on left siblings.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            25.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Common Code Optimizations & Basic Blocks\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Basic Block:</b> A sequence of consecutive statements with one entry point and one exit point.<br/>• <b>Leaders in 3AC:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Statement 1 is a leader.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Target of any conditional/unconditional jump (GOTO) is a leader.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Statement immediately following any jump is a leader.<br/>• <b>Optimization Types:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Constant Folding:</b> Compute constant expressions at compile time (e.g. <code>x = 3 * 7</code> becomes <code>x = 21</code>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Constant Propagation:</b> Replace variable with its known constant value.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dead Code Elimination:</b> Remove code whose result is never used or statements that are never reached.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Strength Reduction:</b> Replace expensive operations with cheaper ones (e.g. <code>x * 2</code> replaced by <code>x << 1</code> or <code>x + x</code>).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Strength reduction replaces an expensive operation with a cheaper one (e.g. multiplication replaced by addition in loops). It does NOT eliminate code!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Identifying Leaders and Basic Blocks\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given 3-Address Code: (1) i = 1; (2) j = 1; (3) t1 = 10 * i; (4) t2 = t1 + j; (5) if t2 < 100 goto (3); (6) goto (1). How many basic blocks are there?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Statement (1) is a leader (first statement).<br/>• Step 2: Target of goto (3) is statement (3) ⇒ (3) is a leader.<br/>• Step 3: Target of goto (1) is statement (1) (already a leader).<br/>• Step 4: Statement following jump (5) is statement (6) ⇒ (6) is a leader.<br/>• Step 5: Leaders are statements (1), (3), and (6).<br/>• Step 6: Basic blocks: Block 1: [(1), (2)]; Block 2: [(3), (4), (5)]; Block 3: [(6)].\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total Basic Blocks = 3.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In L-attributed SDT, actions can be placed anywhere, but they can only use values from elements to their LEFT!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Three-address code has at most 3 addresses per instruction (e.g. x = y op z).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Inheritance Tree & Dead Code Cleanup\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • S-attributed: Like child elements calculating their own weights and reporting them UP to the parent node (Bottom-Up).\n• L-attributed: Like parents sharing an inheritance with children from left to right.\n• Dead Code: Removing unused furniture from a room so you never spend energy cleaning it!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('OS', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            26.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            The fork() System Call & Process Tree\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <code>fork()</code> clones the calling process to create an exact child process.<br/>• <b>Return Value of fork():</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>0 to the Child process</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>Child\'s PID (> 0) to the Parent process</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>negative</b> on failure.<br/>• <b>Number of Processes Created:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>fork()</code> is executed <b>n times</b> sequentially: Total processes = <b>2^n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Number of <b>Child processes</b> created = <b>2^n - 1</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In short-circuit expressions like fork() && fork(): the second fork executes ONLY in the parent process because fork() returns 0 (False) to the child, short-circuiting the &&!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            26.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            CPU Scheduling Metrics & Formulas\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Turnaround Time (TAT):</b> Completion Time - Arrival Time.<br/>• <b>Waiting Time (WT):</b> Turnaround Time - Burst Time.<br/>• <b>Response Time:</b> First Time CPU is Allocated - Arrival Time.<br/>• <b>Key Algorithm Takeaways:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>SJF / SRTF:</b> Provably gives <b>minimum Average Waiting Time</b> among all scheduling algorithms!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Round Robin (RR):</b> Time quantum q. If q is very large, RR degenerates into FCFS; if q is tiny, context switch overhead dominates.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Convoy Effect:</b> Occurs in FCFS when a long CPU-bound process holds the CPU while short I/O-bound processes wait.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: SRTF (Shortest Remaining Time First) is preemptive SJF and gives minimum average waiting time, but can cause starvation for long processes!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Calculating Number of Created Processes\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> How many total child processes are created by the code: for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { fork(); } ?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Loop runs 3 times, executing fork() 3 times unconditionally.<br/>• Step 2: Formula for total processes = 2^n where n = 3 ⇒ 2^3 = 8 total processes.<br/>• Step 3: Total child processes = 2^n - 1 = 8 - 1 = 7 child processes.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Total child processes created = 7.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Do not confuse total processes (2^n) with total child processes (2^n - 1)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Non-preemptive algorithms NEVER interrupt a process once it is running on the CPU.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Cooking Multiple Dishes on a 1-Burner Stove\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • CPU Scheduling: Like a chef with 1 stove burner (CPU) cooking 4 dishes (processes). FCFS finishes dish 1 completely before starting dish 2. Round Robin cooks each dish for 2 minutes, switching rapidly so no customer starves!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('OS', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            27.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Critical Section Requirements & Semaphores\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Critical Section (CS) Problem:</b> Region of code accessing shared resources.<br/>• <b>3 Mandatory Requirements:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Mutual Exclusion (Mandatory):</b> If process Pi is executing in CS, no other process can enter CS.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Progress (Mandatory):</b> Only processes currently competing to enter CS can participate in deciding who enters next.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Bounded Waiting:</b> A bound must exist on the number of times others enter CS before a waiting process gets its turn.<br/>• <b>Counting Semaphores:</b> Initialized to N (available resources).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>wait(S)</code> (or P): decrements S. If S < 0, process blocks.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>signal(S)</code> (or V): increments S. If S ≤ 0, unblocks a waiting process.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If counting semaphore is initialized to S0, after p wait operations and v signal operations, final value = S0 - p + v. If final value is negative (-k), exactly k processes are blocked in the waiting queue!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            27.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Deadlock: 4 Necessary Conditions & Banker\'s Algorithm\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>The 4 Coffman Conditions (Must ALL hold simultaneously for deadlock):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Mutual Exclusion:</b> Resources cannot be shared.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Hold and Wait:</b> Process holds at least 1 resource while requesting others.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>No Preemption:</b> Resources cannot be forcibly taken away.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Circular Wait:</b> A closed loop of processes waiting for resources held by the next.<br/>• <b>Banker\'s Algorithm:</b> Deadlock avoidance via Safe State analysis.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Need Matrix = Max Requirement - Current Allocation</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If available resources can satisfy Need of a process, it finishes and returns its allocation to Available.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An unsafe state does NOT mean deadlock! Every deadlocked state is unsafe, but an unsafe state may or may not lead to deadlock.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Counting Semaphore Value and Blocked Processes\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A counting semaphore S is initialized to 10. Then 14 wait() operations and 3 signal() operations are executed on S. What is the final value of S, and how many processes are blocked?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Formula: Final S = Initial + Signals - Waits.<br/>• Step 2: Final S = 10 + 3 - 14 = 13 - 14 = -1.<br/>• Step 3: When S is negative, the magnitude |S| represents the number of blocked processes.<br/>• Step 4: | -1 | = 1 process is blocked.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Final value of S = -1; Exactly 1 process is blocked.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Peterson\'s algorithm works strictly for TWO processes, not general n processes without modifications!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Strict alternation (using only turn variable) satisfies Mutual Exclusion, but FAILS Progress.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Single-Key Restroom & Traffic Gridlock\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Semaphore: A single restroom key at a gas station. Only the person holding the key can enter (Mutual Exclusion). Others must wait until the key is returned (Wait/Signal).\n• Deadlock: A 4-way intersection gridlock where every car is blocked by the car in front of it, and no one can move forward!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('OS', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            28.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Paging & Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with TLB\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Virtual Address Split:</b> [ Page Number (p) | Page Offset (d) ].<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Page Offset = <b>log2(Page Size)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Page Number = Virtual Address Bits - Page Offset.<br/>• <b>Page Table Size:</b> (Number of Pages) × (Page Table Entry PTE Size).<br/>• <b>Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with 1-level Paging:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>EMAT = Hit_Rate × (t_{tlb} + t_m) + (1 - Hit_Rate) × (t_{tlb} + 2 × t_m)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If TLB miss searches memory directly: EMAT = t_{tlb} + t_m + (1 - Hit_Rate) × t_m.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On a TLB hit, memory is accessed ONCE (for the data word). On a TLB miss in 1-level paging, memory is accessed TWICE (first for page table entry, second for actual data word)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            28.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Page Replacement Algorithms & Belady\'s Anomaly\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>FIFO (First-In First-Out):</b> Replaces the oldest loaded page.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Suffers from <b>Belady\'s Anomaly</b>: Increasing the number of page frames can INCREASE the number of page faults!<br/>• <b>LRU (Least Recently Used):</b> Replaces page not used for the longest time in the past. (Does NOT suffer from Belady\'s Anomaly; belongs to Stack Algorithms).<br/>• <b>Optimal Page Replacement (OPT / MIN):</b> Replaces page that will not be used for longest time in the FUTURE. (Provably minimum page faults, but impossible to implement in practice).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Belady\'s Anomaly occurs in FIFO! Stack algorithms like LRU and Optimal NEVER suffer from Belady\'s Anomaly.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            28.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Unix Inodes & Maximum File Size\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • An Inode contains direct and indirect disk block pointers:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Direct Pointers (e.g. 12 pointers):</b> 12 × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Single Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size) × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Double Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size)² × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Triple Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size)³ × Block Size.<br/>• <b>Max File Size = Sum of all addressable bytes across direct, single, double, and triple pointers!</b>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Number of pointers per indirect block = Block Size / Disk Pointer Size. For 1 KB blocks and 4-byte pointers: 1024 / 4 = 256 pointers per block!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: EMAT Calculation with TLB\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A system has TLB access time of 20 ns and main memory access time of 100 ns. The TLB hit ratio is 90%. What is the effective memory access time EMAT?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: On TLB hit: access TLB (20 ns) + access Memory for data (100 ns) = 120 ns.<br/>• Step 2: On TLB miss: access TLB (20 ns) + access Memory for Page Table (100 ns) + access Memory for data (100 ns) = 220 ns.<br/>• Step 3: EMAT = (0.90 × 120 ns) + (0.10 × 220 ns).<br/>• Step 4: EMAT = 108 ns + 22 ns = 130 ns.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ EMAT = 130 ns.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In multi-level paging with k levels: On TLB miss, you need k memory accesses for page tables + 1 memory access for data = (k + 1) memory accesses!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Internal fragmentation occurs on the last page of a process; paging completely eliminates external fragmentation.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Book Chapter Index & Speed Dial\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Paging: Like dividing a 500-page novel into 10-page chapters so only the chapter you are currently reading needs to be open on your desk.\n• TLB: Like a smartphone speed-dial list storing your top 8 favorite contacts so you don\'t look up their numbers in the full directory each time.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DB', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            29.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            ER to Relational Table Conversion Rules\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Converting Relationships to Tables:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Many-to-Many (M:N):</b> Requires a separate relationship table whose primary key is the combination of primary keys of both participating entities.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>One-to-Many (1:N):</b> Can be combined into the \'Many\' side table by placing the primary key of the \'1\' side as a Foreign Key (FK).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>One-to-One (1:1):</b> Can combine into either table, or merge both into a single table if total participation holds.<br/>• <b>Weak Entity:</b> Has no primary key of its own; identified using a discriminator (partial key) and owner\'s primary key. Always merged with its identifying relationship into 1 table.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 1:N relationship with partial participation on both sides, minimum number of tables is 2! In M:N relationship, minimum number of tables is ALWAYS 3 (Table A, Table B, and Relationship Table AB).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            29.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Relational Algebra vs SQL Rules\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Relational Algebra Operators:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Selection (σ_condition):</b> Filters rows that satisfy condition.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Projection (π_attributes):</b> Selects specified columns and <b>automatically eliminates duplicates</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Natural Join (⨝):</b> Cartesian product followed by equality selection on common attributes, then projection.<br/>• <b>SQL vs Relational Algebra:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Relational Algebra works on Sets (no duplicates).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- SQL operates on Multi-sets / Bags (duplicates preserved unless <code>DISTINCT</code> is specified).<br/>• <b>Three-Valued Logic in SQL:</b> Comparisons with <code>NULL</code> evaluate to <b>UNKNOWN</b>! <code>WHERE NULL = NULL</code> evaluates to UNKNOWN (returns 0 rows).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: π (projection) in Relational Algebra eliminates duplicates automatically, but SELECT in SQL preserves duplicates unless SELECT DISTINCT is explicitly written!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Minimum Relational Tables for ER Diagram\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> An ER diagram has entities E1, E2, E3. Relationship R1 between E1 and E2 is M:N. Relationship R2 between E2 and E3 is 1:N. How many minimum tables are required in relational schema?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: E1 table is created.<br/>• Step 2: E2 table is created.<br/>• Step 3: M:N relationship R1 requires a separate relationship table (R1_table).<br/>• Step 4: 1:N relationship R2 between E2 (1) and E3 (N): E3 can hold the foreign key of E2. Separate table for R2 is NOT needed.<br/>• Step 5: E3 table is created (with FK of E2).<br/>• Step 6: Total tables: E1, E2, R1_table, E3 = 4 tables.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Minimum number of tables = 4.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In SQL, COUNT(*) counts rows including NULLs; COUNT(column_name) ignores NULL rows!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          HAVING clause filters groups AFTER aggregation, while WHERE filters individual rows BEFORE aggregation.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: University Enrollment & Excel Filters\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • ER Model: Blueprint of a university showing Students, Courses, and Departments with relationships (1:Many, Many:Many).\n• Relational Algebra: Filter (Select σ) picks specific rows; Column picker (Project π) hides unwanted columns; Join combines matching rows.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DB', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            30.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            The Step-by-Step Normal Forms Checklist\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>1NF:</b> Every attribute contains strictly <b>atomic values</b> (no multi-valued or composite attributes).<br/>• <b>2NF:</b> Relation is in 1NF AND contains <b>NO Partial Dependency</b> (no non-prime attribute depends on a proper subset of any candidate key).<br/>• <b>3NF:</b> For every non-trivial FD <b>X → Y</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Either <b>X is a Superkey</b>,<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• OR <b>Y is a Prime Attribute</b> (part of some candidate key).<br/>• <b>BCNF (Boyce-Codd Normal Form):</b> For every non-trivial FD <b>X → Y</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>X MUST BE A SUPERKEY!</b> (Strictly eliminates all transitive and partial dependencies).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 3NF, the RHS (Y) is allowed to be a prime attribute even if LHS (X) is not a superkey! In BCNF, this exception is REMOVED: LHS (X) MUST always be a superkey!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            30.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Decomposition Properties: Lossless & Dependency Preserving\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Lossless Join Test (for binary decomposition R1, R2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;R1 ⨝ R2 is Lossless IF AND ONLY IF common attribute is a superkey of at least one relation:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>(R1 ∩ R2) → R1 &nbsp;OR&nbsp; (R1 ∩ R2) → R2</b>.<br/>• <b>Decomposition Guarantees:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any relation can <b>ALWAYS be decomposed into 3NF</b> that is BOTH Lossless AND Dependency Preserving.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any relation can <b>ALWAYS be decomposed into BCNF</b> that is Lossless, but Dependency Preservation is NOT guaranteed!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: BCNF decomposition guarantees Lossless Join, but may LOSE functional dependencies! 3NF decomposition guarantees BOTH Lossless Join AND Dependency Preservation.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            30.3\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            B-Tree vs B+ Tree Core Properties\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>B-Tree:</b> Keys and data pointers are stored in BOTH internal and leaf nodes.<br/>• <b>B+ Tree:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Data pointers are stored <b>ONLY in Leaf Nodes</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Leaf nodes are connected via a <b>singly/doubly linked list</b> for fast sequential range queries.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Internal nodes contain only search keys and child tree pointers (higher fanout!).<br/>• <b>Node Capacities (Order p):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Internal node holds at most <b>p pointers</b> and <b>p - 1 keys</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Minimum keys in internal node: <b>⌈p / 2⌉ - 1</b> (except root: min 1 key).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In B+ Trees, internal nodes only store routing keys (no data pointers)! Hence internal nodes fit far more keys per disk block than standard B-Trees, keeping tree height very small (typically 3 to 4).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Finding Highest Normal Form\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Relation R(A, B, C, D) with FDs: { AB → C, C → D, D → A }. What is the highest normal form of R?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Compute candidate keys: (AB)+ = {A,B,C,D} ⇒ AB is a key. (BC)+ = {B,C,D,A} ⇒ BC is a key. (BD)+ = {B,D,A,C} ⇒ BD is a key.<br/>• Step 2: Candidate keys are AB, BC, BD. Prime attributes = { A, B, C, D } (all attributes are prime!).<br/>• Step 3: Check 2NF: No non-prime attributes exist ⇒ 2NF holds trivially!<br/>• Step 4: Check 3NF: For FD C → D: C is not superkey, but D is a prime attribute! For FD D → A: D is not superkey, but A is a prime attribute! Since RHS is always prime, 3NF holds!<br/>• Step 5: Check BCNF: In C → D, C is not a superkey. Fails BCNF!\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Highest normal form is 3NF.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          If ALL attributes in a relation are Prime Attributes, the relation is GUARANTEED to be in 3NF!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Order of B-tree p means maximum child pointers; maximum keys is always p - 1.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Student Roll Number & Library Index Tree\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Functional Dependency: Like your passport number uniquely determining your name and date of birth (Passport -> Name, DOB).\n• B+ Tree: The alphabetical thumb-tabs on a physical phonebook or dictionary, allowing you to jump to \'Smith\' with just 3 page flips instead of reading all 50,000 names!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('DB', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            31.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Conflict Serializability & Precedence Graphs\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Two operations are in CONFLICT if:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. They belong to <b>different transactions</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. They access the <b>same data item</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>At least one of them is a WRITE</b> operation (Read-Write, Write-Read, or Write-Write).<br/>• <b>Precedence Graph (Testing Conflict Serializability):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Draw directed edge <b>Ti → Tj</b> if Ti executes a conflicting operation BEFORE Tj.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Testing Rule:</b> Schedule is Conflict Serializable <b>IF AND ONLY IF the precedence graph has NO CYCLES (Acyclic)!</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Equivalent serial order = <b>Topological Sort</b> of the graph.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If precedence graph contains a cycle (e.g. T1 → T2 → T1), it is 100% NOT conflict serializable! A schedule without cycles is always conflict serializable.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            31.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Two-Phase Locking (2PL) Protocol\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Basic 2PL:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Growing Phase:</b> Transaction may acquire locks, but CANNOT release any lock.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Shrinking Phase:</b> Transaction may release locks, but CANNOT acquire any new lock.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Guarantees Conflict Serializability!</b> But DOES NOT prevent deadlocks or cascading aborts.<br/>• <b>Strict 2PL:</b> Holds all EXCLUSIVE (X) write locks until Commit/Abort. (Prevents cascading rollbacks!).<br/>• <b>Rigorous 2PL:</b> Holds ALL locks (shared and exclusive) until Commit/Abort.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Basic 2PL guarantees Conflict Serializability, but is NOT free from Deadlocks! Strict 2PL is Recoverable and Cascading-Free.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Conflict Serializability Cycle Detection\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given schedule S: r1(X), r2(Y), w1(Y), w2(X), c1, c2. Is S conflict serializable?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Conflicting pairs on X: r1(X) by T1 and w2(X) by T2 ⇒ T1 executes before T2 ⇒ Edge T1 → T2.<br/>• Step 2: Conflicting pairs on Y: r2(Y) by T2 and w1(Y) by T1 ⇒ T2 executes before T1 ⇒ Edge T2 → T1.<br/>• Step 3: Graph contains edges T1 → T2 and T2 → T1, forming a directed cycle T1 ⇄ T2.<br/>• Step 4: Since precedence graph has a cycle, S is NOT conflict serializable.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Schedule S is NOT conflict serializable (contains cycle T1 ⇄ T2).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Every conflict serializable schedule is view serializable, but not vice versa! View serializability allows blind writes.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          ACID: Durability guarantees that committed changes survive power failures using Write-Ahead Logging (WAL).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: ATM Cash Withdrawal & Bank Balance Consistency\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Atomicity: If an ATM dispenses ₹5,000 cash but power fails before updating your account balance, the system automatically rolls back so your money isn\'t lost.\n• Conflict Serializability: Making concurrent transactions execute as if they ran one after another in some serial order.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            32.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check) Polynomial Division\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Let generator polynomial G(x) have degree <b>k</b> (i.e. k + 1 bits).<br/>• <b>Encoding Steps:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Append <b>k zeros</b> to the end of the original data frame.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Perform <b>Modulo-2 Binary Division</b> (using XOR instead of subtraction).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. The k-bit remainder is the <b>CRC Checksum</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Transmitted frame = <b>Original Data + CRC Remainder</b>.<br/>• <b>Receiver Check:</b> Receiver divides received frame by G(x). If remainder is all 0s, frame is accepted.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If generator polynomial has degree k, append exactly k zeros to data! Modulo-2 division uses XOR (0⊕0=0, 1⊕1=0, 0⊕1=1, 1⊕0=1). Never use arithmetic borrowing!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            32.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Hamming Code & Error Correction\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Parity Bits Formula:</b> For m data bits and p parity bits:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>2^p ≥ m + p + 1</b>.<br/>• Parity bits placed at powers of 2 (positions 1, 2, 4, 8, ...).<br/>• <b>Hamming Distance (d):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• To detect up to <b>d errors</b>: Minimum Hamming distance = <b>d + 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• To correct up to <b>t errors</b>: Minimum Hamming distance = <b>2t + 1</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To correct t single-bit errors, minimum distance d_min ≥ 2t + 1! To detect t errors, d_min ≥ t + 1.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Calculating CRC Transmitted Frame\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Given Data = 100100 and Generator polynomial G(x) = x³ + x² + 1 (binary 1101). Find the CRC remainder and transmitted bit sequence.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Degree of G(x) is 3 ⇒ Append 3 zeros to data: 100100 000.<br/>• Step 2: Divide 100100000 by 1101 using Modulo-2 XOR division:<br/>•   100100000 XOR 110100000 = 010000000<br/>•   010000000 XOR 011010000 = 001010000<br/>•   001010000 XOR 001101000 = 000111000<br/>•   000111000 XOR 000110100 = 000001100<br/>•   000001100 XOR 000001101 = 000000001<br/>• Step 3: 3-bit remainder is 001.<br/>• Step 4: Transmitted codeword = 100100 001.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ CRC remainder = 001; Transmitted frame = 100100001.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In bit stuffing: Sender inserts a \'0\' after FIVE consecutive 1s! Flag is 01111110 (six 1s).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Physical layer deals with bits, Data Link with frames, Network with packets, Transport with segments.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Postal Mail Envelope & Barcode Checksum\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Layered Architecture: Writing a letter (Application), translating it to Spanish (Presentation), putting it in an envelope (Transport), writing address (Network), placing in delivery truck (Data Link), sending along highway (Physical).\n• CRC: The checksum barcode on the back of an envelope that detects if a digit got smudged in the rain.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            33.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Sliding Window Protocol Efficiency Formulas\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Key Parameters:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Transmission Time: <b>Tt = Frame Size (L) / Bandwidth (B)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Propagation Time: <b>Tp = Distance (d) / Propagation Velocity (v)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Parameter a: <b>a = Tp / Tt</b>.<br/>• <b>Efficiency Formulas:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Stop-and-Wait:</b> <b>η = 1 / (1 + 2a)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Sliding Window with window size W:</b> <b>η = W / (1 + 2a)</b> (capped at 100%).<br/>• <b>Window Sizes for k-bit sequence numbers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Go-Back-N (GBN):</b> Sender Window Ws = <b>2^k - 1</b>; Receiver Window Wr = <b>1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Selective Repeat (SR):</b> Sender Window Ws = <b>2^(k - 1)</b>; Receiver Window Wr = <b>2^(k - 1)</b>.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Selective Repeat (SR), Ws + Wr ≤ 2^k. Since Ws = Wr, maximum window size is 2^(k-1)! In GBN, Ws ≤ 2^k - 1 and Wr = 1.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            33.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            CSMA/CD Minimum Frame Size\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Collision Detection Condition:</b> Transmission time MUST be at least twice propagation time:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Tt ≥ 2 × Tp</b>.<br/>• <b>Minimum Frame Size Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>L_min ≥ 2 × Tp × Bandwidth</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;= <b>2 × (Distance / Velocity) × Bandwidth</b>.<br/>• <b>Why 2 × Tp?</b> Because in the worst case, a collision can occur just before the signal reaches the destination, requiring another Tp for the collision signal to travel back to the sender!\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In CSMA/CD, L_min = 2 × Tp × Bandwidth! If frame is smaller than L_min, sender finishes transmitting before collision signal arrives, failing to detect collision.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: CSMA/CD Minimum Frame Length\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A 1 km network has bandwidth 1 Gbps and signal velocity 2 × 10^8 m/s. Calculate the minimum frame size for CSMA/CD.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Distance d = 1000 m; Velocity v = 2 × 10^8 m/s.<br/>• Step 2: Tp = d / v = 1000 / (2 × 10^8) = 5 × 10^(-6) seconds = 5 µs.<br/>• Step 3: Bandwidth B = 1 Gbps = 10^9 bits/sec.<br/>• Step 4: L_min = 2 × Tp × B = 2 × (5 × 10^(-6)) × 10^9 = 10 × 10^3 = 10,000 bits = 1250 bytes.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Minimum frame size = 10,000 bits (1250 bytes).</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In GBN, out-of-order packets are silently discarded because receiver window size is strictly 1!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In Selective Repeat, independent acknowledgments are sent for each packet, requiring memory buffers at receiver.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Walkie-Talkie & Crowded Dinner Party\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Stop-and-Wait: Like a walkie-talkie conversation. You say \'Over\' and wait until the other person acknowledges before speaking again.\n• CSMA/CD: Like people at a dinner party. You listen before speaking (Carrier Sense). If two speak at once (Collision), both stop, wait a random time (Backoff), and try again.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            34.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            IPv4 Subnetting & Usable Hosts\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • In CIDR notation <b>a.b.c.d / n</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Network bits = <b>n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Host bits = <b>32 - n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Total IP addresses = <b>2^(32 - n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Usable Host addresses = 2^(32 - n) - 2</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(Subtract 2: First is Network ID; Last is Directed Broadcast Address).<br/>• <b>Subnet Mask:</b> A 32-bit number with n leading 1s followed by (32 - n) zeros.<br/>• <b>Finding Subnet ID:</b> Bitwise AND between IP address and Subnet Mask.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a /30 subnet: 2^(32-30) = 4 IPs. Minus 2 = exactly 2 usable host addresses (ideal for point-to-point router links)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            34.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Fragmentation Offset Calculation Rules\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU):</b> Max packet size physical link can carry.<br/>• <b>Key Fragmentation Header Fields:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>MF (More Fragments) Flag:</b> 1 for all fragments EXCEPT the last fragment (MF = 0 for last).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>DF (Don\'t Fragment) Flag:</b> If 1 and packet exceeds MTU, router drops packet and sends ICMP error.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Fragment Offset:</b> Offset field in IPv4 header is measured in units of <b>8 bytes</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Offset = (Byte offset of data) / 8</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Data payload in each fragment (except last) MUST be a multiple of 8 bytes!</b>\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fragment offset is measured in units of 8 BYTES! Subtract the 20-byte IP header first: if MTU = 1500 bytes, max data payload is 1480 bytes (1480 / 8 = 185, a valid multiple of 8)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: IPv4 Packet Fragmentation\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> An IP datagram of 4000 bytes (including 20-byte IP header) arrives at a router where MTU = 1500 bytes. Determine the payload size, MF flag, and offset of each fragment.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Total data to transmit = 4000 - 20 = 3980 bytes.<br/>• Step 2: MTU = 1500 ⇒ Max payload per fragment = 1500 - 20 = 1480 bytes (multiple of 8).<br/>• Step 3: Fragment 1: Payload = 1480 bytes, Offset = 0 / 8 = 0, MF = 1.<br/>• Step 4: Fragment 2: Payload = 1480 bytes, Offset = 1480 / 8 = 185, MF = 1.<br/>• Step 5: Remaining data = 3980 - (1480 + 1480) = 1020 bytes.<br/>• Step 6: Fragment 3: Payload = 1020 bytes, Offset = 2960 / 8 = 370, MF = 0.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Frag 1: 1480B, MF=1, Off=0; Frag 2: 1480B, MF=1, Off=185; Frag 3: 1020B, MF=0, Off=370.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Reassembly of fragments is performed ONLY at the DESTINATION host, never at intermediate routers!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Distance Vector routing suffers from Count-to-Infinity problem; solved by Split Horizon and Poison Reverse.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Splitting a Pizza & Oversized Cargo Slicing\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Subnetting: Slicing a large pizza into smaller slices so each department gets exactly what they need without wasting slices.\n• Fragmentation: Like shipping a large sofa that cannot fit through the front door (MTU), so you unscrew it into 3 smaller pieces and reassemble inside!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('CN', 3): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            35.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            TCP Congestion Window Phases\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>1. Slow Start Phase:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Congestion Window (cwnd) starts at 1 MSS (Maximum Segment Size).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- For every ACK received, cwnd increases by 1 MSS (effectively <b>DOUBLES every RTT</b>: exponential growth).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Continues until cwnd reaches <b>ssthresh (slow-start threshold)</b>.<br/>• <b>2. Congestion Avoidance Phase:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- cwnd increases by <b>1 MSS per RTT</b> (additive increase: linear growth).<br/>• <b>3. On Loss Event:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Timeout (Severe Loss):</b> <b>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</b>, and <b>cwnd resets to 1 MSS</b> (re-enters Slow Start).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>3 Duplicate ACKs (Mild Loss - Fast Retransmit):</b> <b>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</b>, and <b>cwnd = ssthresh</b> (Fast Recovery).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On 3 duplicate ACKs, cwnd drops to ssthresh (not 1 MSS!) and enters Congestion Avoidance. On Timeout, cwnd resets all the way down to 1 MSS!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            35.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Application Layer Protocols Summary\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>DNS (Port 53):</b> Uses <b>UDP</b> for standard queries (fast); uses <b>TCP</b> for zone transfers.<br/>• <b>HTTP (Port 80) / HTTPS (Port 443):</b> Uses <b>TCP</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Non-persistent HTTP/1.0: 2 RTTs per object.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Persistent HTTP/1.1: 1 RTT per object after initial connection handshake.<br/>• <b>Email Protocols:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>SMTP (Port 25):</b> Push protocol (client to mail server, server to server).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>POP3 (Port 110) / IMAP (Port 143):</b> Pull protocols (client downloading mail from server).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: SMTP is a PUSH protocol! POP3 and IMAP are PULL protocols! DNS uses UDP for queries but TCP for zone transfers.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Tracing TCP cwnd Growth\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> A TCP connection has ssthresh = 16 KB and MSS = 2 KB. Starting from cwnd = 2 KB, trace the value of cwnd after 5 RTTs (assuming no loss).\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Round 0: cwnd = 2 KB.<br/>• Step 2: RTT 1 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 4 KB.<br/>• Step 3: RTT 2 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 8 KB.<br/>• Step 4: RTT 3 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 16 KB (reaches ssthresh).<br/>• Step 5: RTT 4 (Congestion Avoidance): cwnd increases linearly by 1 MSS (+2 KB) = 18 KB.<br/>• Step 6: RTT 5 (Congestion Avoidance): cwnd increases by 1 MSS (+2 KB) = 20 KB.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ After 5 RTTs, cwnd = 20 KB.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          TCP uses byte sequence numbers, NOT packet sequence numbers! If packet carries 100 bytes starting at seq 500, next packet has seq 600.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Silently discarding packets by UDP provides zero flow or congestion control.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Highway Speed Limit & Highway Traffic Jam\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • TCP Slow Start: Starting your car gently and doubling speed every block until you hit the threshold speed limit.\n• Congestion Avoidance: Once past the threshold, increasing speed by only 1 km/h each block to avoid crashing into traffic ahead!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('GA', 0): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            36.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Time, Speed, Distance & Relative Speed\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Fundamental Formula:</b> <b>Distance = Speed × Time</b>.<br/>• <b>Unit Conversion:</b> <b>1 km/h = 5 / 18 m/s</b> &nbsp;and&nbsp; 1 m/s = 18 / 5 km/h.<br/>• <b>Average Speed:</b> <b>Total Distance / Total Time</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If equal distances covered at speeds v1 and v2: <b>Average Speed = 2 · v1 · v2 / (v1 + v2)</b> (Harmonic Mean!).<br/>• <b>Relative Speed:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Moving in <b>Opposite directions:</b> Relative Speed = <b>v1 + v2</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Moving in <b>Same direction:</b> Relative Speed = <b>|v1 - v2|</b>.<br/>• <b>Boats and Streams:</b> Downstream speed = <b>u + v</b>; Upstream speed = <b>u - v</b> (where u = boat speed, v = stream speed).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Average speed is NEVER the simple arithmetic mean (v1 + v2)/2! For equal distance intervals, average speed is always 2·v1·v2 / (v1 + v2).</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            36.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Time & Work (The LCM Efficiency Method)\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>LCM Method:</b> Assume Total Work = <b>LCM of all individual completion times</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Calculate 1-day efficiency = Total Work / Individual Days.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Combined efficiency = Sum of individual efficiencies.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Total Days = Total Work / Combined Efficiency.<br/>• <b>Pipes and Cisterns:</b> Inlet pipe adds positive work (+); Outlet / leak pipe subtracts negative work (-).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Using the LCM method eliminates messy fractions completely! If A takes 12 days and B takes 18 days: Total Work = LCM(12, 18) = 36 units. Efficiency of A = 3, B = 2. Together = 36 / 5 = 7.2 days!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Two Trains Crossing Each Other\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Two trains of lengths 120 m and 180 m travel in opposite directions on parallel tracks at 40 km/h and 50 km/h. How many seconds do they take to completely pass each other?\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Total distance to cover = Sum of train lengths = 120 + 180 = 300 meters.<br/>• Step 2: Relative speed in opposite directions = 40 + 50 = 90 km/h.<br/>• Step 3: Convert speed to m/s: 90 × (5 / 18) = 25 m/s.<br/>• Step 4: Time taken = Distance / Speed = 300 m / (25 m/s) = 12 seconds.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Time taken = 12 seconds.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          When a train crosses a pole or person, distance covered = length of train alone (pole has negligible width).\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          Successive percentage discount of x% and y% is NOT (x + y)%! Equivalent discount = x + y - (xy / 100)%.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Two Taps Filling a Bathtub\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Work and Time: Tap A fills a tub in 10 minutes (rate = 1/10 per min); Tap B fills it in 15 minutes (rate = 1/15 per min). Working together, their speeds add up: 1/10 + 1/15 = 1/6, filling the tub in 6 minutes!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('GA', 1): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            37.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Clock Angle Formula\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • Angle between Hour and Minute hand at H hours and M minutes:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Angle θ = | 30 × H - (11 / 2) × M |</b>.<br/>• <b>Speeds of Hands:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Minute hand speed: <b>6° per minute</b> (360° / 60 min).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Hour hand speed: <b>0.5° per minute</b> (30° / 60 min).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Relative speed: <b>5.5° per minute</b>.<br/>• <b>Coincidence (0°):</b> Hands coincide <b>22 times in 24 hours</b> (not 24 times, due to 11-12-1 overlap).\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Use θ = |30H - 5.5M|! If angle is greater than 180°, reflex angle is 360° - θ.</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            37.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Painted Cube Slicing Formulas\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • A large cube painted on all outside faces is cut into <b>n³ smaller equal cubes</b> (n cuts per edge):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>3 Faces Painted (Corners):</b> ALWAYS exactly <b>8 cubes</b> (for any n ≥ 2).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>2 Faces Painted (Edges):</b> <b>12 × (n - 2)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>1 Face Painted (Face Centers):</b> <b>6 × (n - 2)²</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>0 Faces Painted (Inner Core):</b> <b>(n - 2)³</b>.<br/>• <b>Verification:</b> 8 + 12(n - 2) + 6(n - 2)² + (n - 2)³ = n³.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Regardless of the value of n, cubes with 3 faces painted is ALWAYS 8 (the 8 corners of the cube)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Angle between Clock Hands\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Find the angle between the hour hand and minute hand of a clock at 3:40.\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Here H = 3, M = 40.<br/>• Step 2: Formula: θ = | 30 × H - (11 / 2) × M |.<br/>• Step 3: 30 × 3 = 90.<br/>• Step 4: (11 / 2) × 40 = 11 × 20 = 220.<br/>• Step 5: θ = | 90 - 220 | = | -130 | = 130°.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Angle between hands = 130°.</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In calendar problems, remember that century years like 1900 are NOT leap years unless divisible by 400 (2000 is a leap year)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In blood relations, never assume gender from a person\'s name unless explicitly stated or implied by relationship (e.g. \'sister\', \'father\').\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: Rubik\'s Cube Slicing & Wall Clock Hands\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Painted Cube: Like a 3x3x3 Rubik\'s cube painted red on outside. Only the 8 corner pieces have 3 painted faces! Center pieces inside the cube have 0 painted faces.\n• Clock Hands: At 12:00 both hands point straight up. By 12:30, the hour hand has already crept halfway toward 1 (15 degrees)!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>', ('GA', 2): '<div class="master-chapter-textbook-container space-y-8">\n  <!-- SECTION 1: CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE) -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>1. CORE CONCEPTUAL FOUNDATIONS (POINT-BY-POINT STUDY GUIDE)</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Crystal-clear, point-by-point explanations of core concepts with step-by-step logic, code, and key exam rules:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-4">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            38.1\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Subject-Verb Agreement (Concord) Rules\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Rule 1: Either/Or & Neither/Nor:</b> Verb agrees with the <b>CLOSER subject</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: \'Neither the manager nor the <i>employees were</i> present.\'<br/>• <b>Rule 2: As well as / Together with / Along with:</b> Verb agrees with the <b>FIRST subject</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: \'The <i>captain</i>, along with his crew members, <b>was</b> honored.\'<br/>• <b>Rule 3: Each, Every, Everyone, Neither (alone):</b> Strictly <b>SINGULAR</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: \'Each of the students <b>has</b> completed the project.\'\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In \'A as well as B\', verb follows A! In \'Either A or B\', verb follows B (the nearest subject)!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span class="text-xs font-mono font-extrabold px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800">\n            38.2\n          </span>\n          <h3 class="text-sm sm:text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">\n            Common Word Confusions in GATE\n          </h3>\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed space-y-2">\n          • <b>Affect vs Effect:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Affect (Verb):</b> To influence. (\'The weather affected his mood\').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Effect (Noun):</b> The result/consequence. (\'The policy had a good effect\').<br/>• <b>Complement vs Compliment:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Complement:</b> Completes or enhances something (\'Bread and butter complement each other\').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Compliment:</b> An expression of praise (\'He paid her a nice compliment\').<br/>• <b>Principal vs Principle:</b> Principal = head of school / main; Principle = fundamental truth / rule.\n        </div>\n        \n        <div class="mt-3 p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50/90 dark:bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-300/80 dark:border-emerald-700/60 text-emerald-950 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed font-semibold flex items-start gap-2 shadow-2xs">\n          <span>✨</span>\n          <div>🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Affect is almost always an Action (Verb), Effect is an End-result (Noun). Remember RAVEN: Remember Affect Verb Effect Noun!</div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2: STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES -->\n  <div class="space-y-4">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>2. STEP-BY-STEP EXAM WORKED EXAMPLES & PROBLEM TRACES</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        High-yield exam-pattern numerical and conceptual problems solved with explicit step-by-step derivations:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/80 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-3">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-purple-100 dark:bg-purple-950/80 text-purple-800 dark:text-purple-300 border border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800/60">\n          <span>🔢 Step-by-Step Solved Problem</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Worked Example: Subject-Verb Agreement Selection\n        </h4>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-3.5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">\n          <b>Problem Statement:</b> Choose the correct verb: \'Neither the professor nor his research students _____ (was / were) able to solve the puzzle.\'\n        </div>\n        <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed pt-1 space-y-1.5">\n          <b>Step-by-Step Solution & Traces:</b><br/>\n          • Step 1: Identify conjunction: \'Neither ... nor ...\'.<br/>• Step 2: Rule of Proximity: The verb agrees with the subject closer to it.<br/>• Step 3: The closer subject is \'his research students\' (Plural).<br/>• Step 4: Plural subject requires plural verb \'were\'.\n        </div>\n        <div class="p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/50 border border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-700 text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 text-xs sm:text-sm font-bold flex items-center gap-2">\n          <span>✅ Correct sentence: \'Neither the professor nor his research students were able to solve the puzzle.\'</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3: COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>3. COMMON MISCONCEPTIONS & EXAM TRAP WARNINGS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Critical negative-marking traps designed to catch unprepared candidates:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-2.5">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          In reading comprehension, answers that use extreme words like \'always\', \'never\', \'only\' are usually wrong unless explicitly in the text!\n        </p>\n      </div>\n      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/30 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60 space-y-1.5">\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 text-xs sm:text-sm flex items-center gap-1.5">\n          <span>⚠️ GATE Exam Pitfall</span>\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">\n          An assumption is an UNSTATED premise required for the conclusion to hold true.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4: QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS -->\n  <div class="space-y-3">\n    <div class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 pb-2">\n      <h2 class="text-sm sm:text-base md:text-lg font-black text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 tracking-wide uppercase flex items-center gap-2">\n        <span>4. QUICK INTUITIVE MENTAL MODELS</span>\n      </h2>\n      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-0.5">\n        Simple everyday analogies to connect technical concepts with natural intuition:\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class="space-y-3">\n      \n      <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50/90 dark:bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-200/80 dark:border-slate-700/70 shadow-xs space-y-2">\n        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-bold bg-amber-100 dark:bg-amber-950/80 text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800/60">\n          <span>💡 Intuitive Mental Model</span>\n        </div>\n        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 text-sm sm:text-base">\n          Real-Life Analogy: The Orchestra Conductor & Courtroom Logic\n        </h4>\n        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line">\n          • Concord: The subject and verb must play in harmony (Singular subject takes singular verb).\n• Critical Reasoning: Distinguishing between a confirmed fact stated in a text versus an unproven assumption made by the author.\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>'}
